{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_2","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc 'nl -ba docs/design/yin.vm.code-as-tuples.md'","aggregated_output":"     1\t# yin.vm.code-as-tuples — code as content-addressed tuples\n     2\t\n     3\tStatus: Proposed, revision 8 (after the owner's 2026-09-15 clarification\n     4\tof ruling 1, accepted through an APPROVE-WITH-FINDINGS r7 review). This\n     5\tdocument implements eight owner rulings — seven of 2026-09-14 and one of\n     6\t2026-09-15: the map AST is the Universal AST's canonical representation that gets evaluated by the ast-walker (a LISP using maps, not a LISP using vectors instead of lists). However, the Universal AST is converted into flat per-node tuple rows (`[id tag & slots]`) that act like bytecode, preserving everything in the Universal AST so that the semantic VM can execute it quickly because of the linearization of the AST into tuples (the VM lowers these rows into an instruction vector for execution). These rows are what is content-hashed, stored, streamed, and named — there is no nested tuple tree (ruling 1 of 2026-09-14,\n     7\tclarified 2026-09-15); the grammar is fixed-arity, saturated, and\n     8\tprovenance-free; `dao.space.query/q` runs over the rows directly; all `yin.vm` evaluators are `dao.stream` observers that load rows to evaluate code; concurrently, a dedicated AST indexer observes the same stream and maintains the **row relation** of §6.1 (the `$ast` relation `q` runs over) from the rows it observes. `dao.jing` is the content store the rows already live in; it is not the indexer's output; code on a stream is rows end to end; `t` and `m` live in a ledger of\n     9\trecords over code addresses; datoms `[e a v t m]` are the reference\n    10\tlayer; and the round-trip law — `map → rows → map` and `rows → map →\n    11\trows` are identities — is a first-class conformance obligation (ruling 8,\n    12\t2026-09-15). The rulings are inputs. What follows is their\n    13\timplementation as a protocol: every sentence is a rule of the design, and\n    14\teach call it makes is stated once.\n    15\t\n    16\tThis document is subordinate to\n    17\t[`datom.world.md`](./datom.world.md) (axioms and invariants),\n    18\t[`datom.md`](./datom.md) (the datom contract: stream-local `e`/`t`/`m`,\n    19\tforeign references only in `v`, reserved markers),\n    20\t[`yin.vm.semantic.md`](./yin.vm.semantic.md) (the segment, its opcode table\n    21\t§2.4, well-formedness §2.6, lowering §5),\n    22\t[`yin.vm.universal-continuation-format.md`](./yin.vm.universal-continuation-format.md)\n    23\t(UCF: the canonical instruction vector §7.3.2, contract stamp §7.3.3,\n    24\tprimitive profiles §7.5.2, dependency closure §7.6.1, custody §7.7),\n    25\t[`yin.vm.macro.md`](./yin.vm.macro.md) (admission, harvest, and the\n    26\texpansion event), [`dao.jing.md`](./dao.jing.md) (content addressing and\n    27\tits open canonical encoding), and\n    28\t[`dao.space.query.md`](./dao.space.query.md) (the positional Datalog\n    29\tsurface and current-state resolution). It extends them; where it restates\n    30\tone of their rules it cites the rule. Where it supersedes one, it says so\n    31\t(§7.2). Line numbers were read from the working tree on 2026-09-14 and\n    32\tre-verified on 2026-09-15.\n    33\t\n    34\t---\n    35\t\n    36\t## §1 Stance and layering\n    37\t\n    38\tCode lives in one layer only, content; the other two layers never hold\n    39\tcode, only facts about it and views of it. Each has one job, and the\n    40\tthings that never cross between them are what make the design hold.\n    41\t\n    42\t+----------------+--------------------------------+-------------------------------------+--------------------+--------------------------+--------------------------------+\n    43\t| Layer          | Example                        | What it holds                       | Identity           | Where it lives           | Mutability                     |\n    44\t+================+================================+=====================================+====================+==========================+================================+\n    45\t| **Content**    | `{:type :lambda :params [x]…}`,| The Map AST code itself, stored as  | the content        | `dao.jing`, under its    | immutable; no `t`, no `m`, no  |\n    46\t|                | the Map AST semantic root      | a linearized bytecode of flat       | address — a row's  | address                  | name, no occurrence, no        |\n    47\t|                |                                | per-node tuple rows (§2) and        | id; a tree is named| (`src/cljc/dao/jing.cljc | predecessor address inside     |\n    48\t|                |                                | instruction vectors (§5); and       | by its root row's  | :217-225`)               | code content                   |\n    49\t|                |                                | also content-addressed values       | id (§4.1)          |                          |                                |\n    50\t+----------------+--------------------------------+-------------------------------------+--------------------+--------------------------+--------------------------------+\n    51\t| **Refs**       | [e :yin/code                   | Datoms `[e a v t m]` whose `v` is a | the datom itself,  | the ledger relation      | append-only; the hash chain is |\n    52\t|                | :segment/sha256-…]             | code or record address: names,      | in a `dao.space`   | (§8), transacted by a    | realized by ledger records and |\n    53\t|                |                                | history, retraction, derivation,    | ledger             | `dao.space` transactor   | the refs that publish them     |\n    54\t|                |                                | expansion, publication, custody     |                    |                          | (§8.2)                         |\n    55\t+----------------+--------------------------------+-------------------------------------+--------------------+--------------------------+--------------------------------+\n    56\t| **Query**      | `[seg 12 :call 2 false]`, one  | Entity-shaped views of code         | derived; never an  | computed from content by | recomputed at will; discarding |\n    57\t|                | segment-qualified row (§6.2)   | internals: the segment rows         | identity           | a pure function, kept by | a projection loses nothing     |\n    58\t|                |                                | `[pc tag & ops]`, the datom         |                    | whoever queries (e.g.    |                                |\n    59\t|                |                                | projection `[e a v t m]` of a tree, |                    | the AST indexer)         |                                |\n    60\t|                |                                | and the occurrence relation (§6.1)  |                    |                          |                                |\n    61\t+----------------+--------------------------------+-------------------------------------+--------------------+--------------------------+--------------------------------+\n    62\t\n    63\tThe layering is the git/Unison one: content is what git calls objects — blobs and\n    64\tterms named by hash — refs are `name → hash` entries with history, and a projection is a\n    65\tworking tree or an index that can be regenerated from the content.\n    66\t\n    67\t**Where the semantic layer lives.** The map AST — a tag plus named fields,\n    68\tthe walker's form — is the language's own representation, and it is a\n    69\tpresentation of the content, not a fourth layer: it is never\n    70\tcontent-hashed, stored, or shipped; it exists on both sides of storage,\n    71\tfrontend-side before the boundary projection and machine-side after\n    72\tload-time reconstruction, ephemeral and per-consumer, like an image\n    73\t(§2.1).\n    74\t\n    75\tThe third layer is named for its job, querying; its members are the\n    76\tprojections — the segment rows (`§6.2`), the datom projection (`§6.5`),\n    77\tand the occurrence relation (`§6.1`) — and wherever this document says\n    78\t\"a projection\" it means one of those forms, never the layer. The flat\n    79\tper-node rows are not among them: a row is the stored form itself, not a\n    80\tview of something else (`§2.1`).\n    81\t\n    82\t**What the datom layer is for.** A datom never holds a node or an\n    83\tinstruction under this design; it holds a fact whose subject or value is an\n    84\taddress. Four jobs remain, and each is a thing an immutable value cannot do:\n    85\t\n    86\t+------------------------+------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n    87\t| Job                    | Why content cannot do it                                                                                                           |\n    88\t+========================+====================================================================================================================================+\n    89\t| naming                 | `[name :yin/code addr]` says what a name currently means; a name is mutable and retractable, content is not                        |\n    90\t+------------------------+------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n    91\t| time and history       | `t` and `m`, assert/retract, as-of views — an immutable value cannot be retracted, so the ledger rides on datoms                   |\n    92\t+------------------------+------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n    93\t| provenance and custody | derive, expand, and publish events, UCF's occurrence ledgers and leases: facts a medium possesses; a hash possesses nothing        |\n    94\t+------------------------+------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n    95\t| indexed storage        | the transactor, btree, and covered indexes, `pull`/entity navigation, attribute-dimension queries (`§6.5`): datom-native machinery |\n    96\t+------------------------+------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n    97\t\n    98\tIn return, datoms no longer do three things: they are not identity (the\n    99\taddress is), not the load path (loaders take content directly, `§7.1`), and\n   100\tnot execution (the hot loop decodes once and never consults a datom,\n   101\t`§7.6`). Code was the special case — the substrate's ordinary business\n   102\t(traces, stream facts, stigmergy, schema, data) is datoms; this design only\n   103\tpulled code out of them and made it properly immutable.\n   104\t\n   105\tSaid plainly: the datom layer is not a description of the code; it is the\n   106\tonly place the code's name, past, and ownership exist. Content says what\n   107\tthe program is; datoms say which program we mean, where it came from, and\n   108\twho may run it.\n   109\t\n   110\tWhat never crosses:\n   111\t\n   112\t- **Nothing about time, naming, occurrence, or provenance enters code\n   113\t  content.** `t`, `m`, entity ids, source positions, the macro flag, the\n   114\t  expansion that produced a tree, the AST a segment came from, and the\n   115\t  address itself are all outside the hashed value. UCF §7.3.2 already\n   116\t  excludes these from the instruction vector; §2.5 applies the same\n   117\t  exclusion to the AST. The consequence is stated, not hidden: code\n   118\t  content does not know its predecessor, so the chain between values is\n   119\t  held by ledger records, never by the values themselves (§8.2).\n   120\t- **No code content enters a ref.** A ref carries an address and nothing\n   121\t  else about the code; two ledgers that agree on `dao.jing` addresses\n   122\t  interoperate without exchanging trees.\n   123\t- **No datom-projection tempid is ever named by a ref, a ledger row, a\n   124\t  closure, or a continuation.** The datom projection's `e` values are\n   125\t  local; only addresses cross layers, and this is what retires the\n   126\t  `:eid` sharing of `src/cljc/yin/vm.cljc:394-411` (§4.4).\n   127\t- **Content identity is not occurrence identity.** An address says *what*\n   128\t  a value is. Which source position, which batch, which call site, which\n   129\t  expansion attempt produced it is occurrence information, and it is kept\n   130\t  in side tables and events keyed by an origin (source batch or producing\n   131\t  event), root address, and structural path (§2.5, §4.4, §8.4), never by\n   132\t  content address alone.\n   133\t- **The hot loop reads content only.** An evaluator builds its image at\n   134\t  load — the walker reconstructs the map AST from rows, the semantic VM\n   135\t  decodes the vector — and never consults a ref or a projection while\n   136\t  stepping (§7.6).\n   137\t\n   138\t---\n   139\t\n   140\t## §2 The AST tuple grammar\n   141\t\n   142\t### 2.1 Form\n   143\t\n   144\tThe map AST is the code's own representation — the **semantic layer** (the Universal AST). A\n   145\tnode is a map of its tag and its named fields:\n   146\t\n   147\t```clojure\n   148\t{:type :lambda,\n   149\t :params [x],\n   150\t :body {:type :application,\n   151\t        :operator {:type :global, :name +},\n   152\t        :operands [{:type :variable, :name x} {:type :literal, :value 1}],\n   153\t        :tail? true}}\n   154\t```\n   155\t\n   156\tThe fields are what the walker dispatches on today (`src/cljc/yin/vm/ast_walker.cljc:333-470`); the walker's `:variable` and `:lambda` arms resolve names against the lexical environment and bind `params`, while `:global` resolves through the store/primitives/modules (`engine.cljc:46-58`).\n   157\t\n   158\tThe map AST is never hashed directly, never stored, never\n   159\tshipped. It exists on both sides of storage — frontend-side, where\n   160\tfrontends emit it, and machine-side, where the loader reconstructs it\n   161\t(§7.1) — and it is ephemeral and per-consumer, like an image: each\n   162\tconsumer's map is its own value, though conceptually they all represent the canonical Universal AST (the semantic representation).\n   163\t\n   164\tThe **canonical stored artifact** is the **flat per-node row projection** of the map\n   165\tAST, `project : map-ast → rows`, one row per node (the bytecode linearization):\n   166\t\n   167\t```clojure\n   168\t[A :lambda 1 B]\n   169\t[B :application C [D E] true]\n   170\t[C :global +]\n   171\t[D :variable 0]\n   172\t[E :literal 1]          ; A..E are :segment/sha256-… row ids\n   173\t```\n   174\t\n   175\tA row's **body** is `[tag & slots]`: the slots are the node's field\n   176\tvalues in the §2.3 order — a child slot holds the child row's id, a\n   177\t`nodes` slot holds the ordered vector of the children's ids, and every\n   178\tother slot holds its data unchanged. The row's id is\n   179\t`(dao.jing/segment-key body)` (`src/cljc/dao/jing.cljc:217-225`). The stored payload in `dao.jing` is exactly the flat body `[tag & slots]`. When queried or streamed, the system reconstructs the full logical row by prepending the returned address, yielding `[id tag & slots]`. The id is an address envelope outside the hashed payload. The\n   180\tid is therefore merkle: a parent's id commits to its children's ids\n   181\tinline, and a tree's address is its root row's id (§4.1). These rows are\n   182\twhat `dao.jing` stores, what streams carry, what addresses name, and\n   183\twhat `q` queries. **There is no nested tuple tree anywhere in this\n   184\tdesign**: the rows are not interleaved into a tree-shaped value, and no\n   185\tsentence below may be read as one; the tree is the map AST the rows\n   186\treconstruct to. Identical subtrees project to the identical row, so a\n   187\tstored set holds one row per distinct subtree and is the tree's DAG\n   188\t(§4.4). Whether `dao.jing` stores rows as individual values (git-style,\n   189\tshared subtrees shared) or packs them per tree is an **Open Question**,\n   190\tnot just an implementation note, because the choice determines the loader's interface (N fetches vs 1 fetch) and the validator's reachability rules. If packed, the pack requires its own address distinct from the root id.\n   191\t\n   192\tThe projection is total and lossless by the round-trip law (§7.2): for\n   193\tevery corpus program, `map → rows → map` is the identity on the\n   194\t*canonical* map AST (post-strip and post-saturate, with frontend `:yang/*` keys and stray `tail?` marks gone), and `rows → map → rows` is the identity on the rows.\n   195\tThe §2.3 table is the dictionary of both directions — a tag's slot list,\n   196\tin order, is the map's key set, so one table fixes the row's positions\n   197\tand the map's fields together.\n   198\t\n   199\t**Runtime `assoc` into a map node is legal.** The node a walker holds\n   200\tunder `:frame` is the machine's ephemeral image of the code, not a\n   201\tcanonical value, and nothing runtime-`assoc`ed is ever projected back to\n   202\trows: the cold `:eval-operator` arm `assoc`s `:operator-evaluated?` and\n   203\t`:fn` into it (`ast_walker.cljc:213-217`), the cold `:eval-operand` arm\n   204\t`assoc`s `:evaluated` (`ast_walker.cljc:226-237`), and the hot arms do\n   205\tthe same (`ast_walker.cljc:532-541`, `:576-580`); all of it stays inside\n   206\tthe machine. What the boundary requires is only that a projection's\n   207\tinput be a well-formed map AST (§2.4, §2.5); the reverse direction never\n   208\tre-derives what evaluation wrote (§2.6).\n   209\t\n   210\t### 2.2 Slot kinds\n   211\t\n   212\tEvery slot in the table has one kind. The kind is fixed by the tag and the\n   213\tposition; it is never inferred from the value. A literal whose value happens\n   214\tto look like a node is a value, which is the rule `linearize/ast-children`\n   215\talready states (`src/cljc/yin/vm/linearize.cljc:230-242`).\n   216\t\n   217\t+---------+----------------------------------+---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n   218\t| Kind    | Admits                           | Notes                                                                                                                     |\n   219\t+=========+==================================+===========================================================================================================================+\n   220\t| `node`  | one child node                   | the child's row id in a row; the child's map in the semantic layer                                                        |\n   221\t+---------+----------------------------------+---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n   222\t| `nodes` | a vector of child nodes,         | one slot, so arity stays fixed however many operands there are; the ordered ids in a row, the maps in order in the        |\n   223\t|         | possibly empty                   | semantic layer                                                                                                            |\n   224\t+---------+----------------------------------+---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n   225\t| `data`  | plain data as                    | never a host function or object                                                                                           |\n   226\t|         | `linearize/plain-data?` defines  |                                                                                                                           |\n   227\t|         | it (`linearize.cljc:45-58`):     |                                                                                                                           |\n   228\t|         | nil, booleans, numbers, strings, |                                                                                                                           |\n   229\t|         | keywords, symbols, and           |                                                                                                                           |\n   230\t|         | collections of them, with plain  |                                                                                                                           |\n   231\t|         | metadata                         |                                                                                                                           |\n   232\t+---------+----------------------------------+---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n   233\t| `key`   | a store key: any value of the    | the store contract is a map lookup with no key restriction (`ast_walker.cljc:401`, `:402-404`); the linearizer admits any |\n   234\t|         | `data` kind                      | `plain-data?` key (`linearize.cljc:79-85`, `:138-141`) and the loader carries it through unchanged                        |\n   235\t|         |                                  | (`src/cljc/yin/vm/semantic.cljc:577-579`), so symbols (`yin/def`), keywords (`test/yin/vm/parity_test.cljc:99`), and|\n   236\t|         |                                  | numbers are all portable keys today. `key` is a named kind rather than `data` only so that the same definition is cited by|\n   237\t|         |                                  | AST validation (§7.4), instruction validation (§7.5), dependency extraction (§7.7), and the store-slice encoding of UCF   |\n   238\t|         |                                  | §7.6.2; it is the `data` domain and nothing narrower                                                                      |\n   239\t+---------+----------------------------------+---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n   240\t| `sym`   | one symbol                       |                                                                                                                           |\n   241\t+---------+----------------------------------+---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n   242\t| `kw`    | one keyword                      |                                                                                                                           |\n   243\t+---------+----------------------------------+---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n   244\t| `str`   | one string                       |                                                                                                                           |\n   245\t+---------+----------------------------------+---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n   246\t| `int`   | one non-negative integer         |                                                                                                                           |\n   247\t+---------+----------------------------------+---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n   248\t| `bool`  | exactly `true` or `false`        | never nil, never absent                                                                                                   |\n   249\t+---------+----------------------------------+---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n   250\t\n   251\t### 2.3 The table\n   252\t\n   253\tThe tag set is the walker's dispatch set reconciled with the codec's\n   254\tinventory (`src/cljc/yin/vm.cljc:413-478`) under the two boundary calls\n   255\tof §3. The `Body arity` column counts the body items (tag plus slots). A full row's count is its body arity plus one (for the ID). This table is the **projection and reconstruction\n   256\tdictionary**: the slot list of a tag, in order, is exactly the map's key\n   257\tset beyond `:type` — the arity ↔ key-set correspondence — so the one\n   258\ttable fixes the row's positions and the map's fields together. Position\n   259\t`i` of a row body holds the value of the field the *Slots* column names\n   260\tat `i`; projection reads the positions, reconstruction reads the names,\n   261\tand neither side may disagree with the other.\n   262\t\n   263\t+-------------------------+------------+--------------------------------------+--------------------------+------------------+--------------------+-----------------+\n   264\t| Tag                     | Body arity | Row body                             | Slots                    | Slot kinds       | Walker arm         | Codec arm       |\n   265\t+=========================+============+======================================+==========================+==================+====================+=================+\n   266\t| :literal                | 2          | [:literal value]                     | value                    | data             | ast_walker.cljc:360| v2.cljc:414-415 |\n   267\t+-------------------------+------------+--------------------------------------+--------------------------+------------------+--------------------+-----------------+\n   268\t| :variable               | 2          | [:variable name]                     | name                     | sym              | changes: index -> name   | changes: `:yin/index` -> `:yin/name` |\n   269\t+-------------------------+------------+--------------------------------------+--------------------------+------------------+--------------------+-----------------+\n   270\t| :global                 | 2          | [:global name]                       | name                     | sym              | added by this design | added by this design |\n   271\t+-------------------------+------------+--------------------------------------+--------------------------+------------------+--------------------+-----------------+\n   272\t| :lambda                 | 3          | [:lambda params body]                | params body              | data, node       | changes: arity -> params | changes: arity -> params |\n   273\t+-------------------------+------------+--------------------------------------+--------------------------+------------------+--------------------+-----------------+\n   274\t| :application            | 4          | [:application operator operands      | operator operands tail?  | node, nodes, bool| :371-376           | :424-429        |\n   275\t|                         |            | tail?]                               |                          |                  |                    |                 |\n   276\t+-------------------------+------------+--------------------------------------+--------------------------+------------------+--------------------+-----------------+\n   277\t| :if                     | 4          | [:if test consequent alternate]      | test consequent alternate| node, node, node | :377-381           | :435-441        |\n   278\t+-------------------------+------------+--------------------------------------+--------------------------+------------------+--------------------+-----------------+\n   279\t| :dao.stream.apply/call  | 3          | [:dao.stream.apply/call op operands] | op operands              | kw, nodes        | :382-394           | :430-434        |\n   280\t+-------------------------+------------+--------------------------------------+--------------------------+------------------+--------------------+-----------------+\n   281\t| :vm/gensym              | 2          | [:vm/gensym prefix]                  | prefix                   | str              | :395-400           | :443-444        |\n   282\t+-------------------------+------------+--------------------------------------+--------------------------+------------------+--------------------+-----------------+\n   283\t| :vm/store-get           | 2          | [:vm/store-get key]                  | key                      | key              | :401               | :445-446        |\n   284\t+-------------------------+------------+--------------------------------------+--------------------------+------------------+--------------------+-----------------+\n   285\t| :vm/store-put           | 3          | [:vm/store-put key val]              | key val                  | key, data        | :402-409           | :447-449        |\n   286\t+-------------------------+------------+--------------------------------------+--------------------------+------------------+--------------------+-----------------+\n   287\t| :vm/current-continuation| 1          | [:vm/current-continuation]           | —                        | —                | :422-427           | :474-475        |\n   288\t+-------------------------+------------+--------------------------------------+--------------------------+------------------+--------------------+-----------------+\n   289\t| :vm/park                | 1          | [:vm/park]                           | —                        | —                | :428-430           | :469            |\n   290\t+-------------------------+------------+--------------------------------------+--------------------------+------------------+--------------------+-----------------+\n   291\t| :vm/resume              | 3          | [:vm/resume parked-id val]           | parked-id val            | kw, node         | :431-438           | :470-473        |\n   292\t+-------------------------+------------+--------------------------------------+--------------------------+------------------+--------------------+-----------------+\n   293\t| :stream/make            | 2          | [:stream/make buffer]                | buffer                   | int              | :439-447           | :451-453        |\n   294\t+-------------------------+------------+--------------------------------------+--------------------------+------------------+--------------------+-----------------+\n   295\t| :stream/put             | 3          | [:stream/put target val]             | target val               | node, node       | :448-454           | :454-458        |\n   296\t+-------------------------+------------+--------------------------------------+--------------------------+------------------+--------------------+-----------------+\n   297\t| :stream/cursor          | 2          | [:stream/cursor source]              | source                   | node             | :455-461           | :459-461        |\n   298\t+-------------------------+------------+--------------------------------------+--------------------------+------------------+--------------------+-----------------+\n   299\t| :stream/next            | 2          | [:stream/next source]                | source                   | node             | :462-468           | :462-464        |\n   300\t+-------------------------+------------+--------------------------------------+--------------------------+------------------+--------------------+-----------------+\n   301\t| :stream/close           | 2          | [:stream/close source]               | source                   | node             | added by §3.2      | :465-467        |\n   302\t+-------------------------+------------+--------------------------------------+--------------------------+------------------+--------------------+-----------------+\n   303\t\n   304\t**Named Variables:** A `:variable` row's `name` retains the original string/symbol from the AST. (De Bruijn computation is deferred to the Register VM phase).\n   305\t\n   306\t`:vm/store-update` is not a tag (§3.1). `:yin/macro-expand` is not a tag:\n   307\tthe walker has no arm for it and the linearizer rejects it\n   308\t(`linearize.cljc:19-22`); expansion happens before code reaches this\n   309\tgrammar (`yin.vm.macro.md`, decision 1).\n   310\t\n   311\tThe `:vm/store-put` value slot is data, not a node: the walker stores\n   312\t`(:val node)` without evaluating it (`ast_walker.cljc:402-404`) and the\n   313\tcodec emits it as `:yin/value` (`v2.cljc:449`). The `:vm/resume` value slot\n   314\tis a node: the walker evaluates it (`ast_walker.cljc:431-438`).\n   315\t\n   316\t**`tail?` is a slot of `:application` only.** The one reader of the tail\n   317\tmark in the whole v2 lineage is the linearizer's `:call` emission\n   318\t(`linearize.cljc:107-108`), which reads it on `:application`; the walker\n   319\tnever reads it; `:ffi-call` has no tail operand (`yin.vm.semantic.md`\n   320\t§2.4). The yang frontends mark `:tail? true` on literals, defs, and other\n   321\tnodes (`src/cljc/yang/clojure.cljc:132`, `:164`; `src/cljc/yang/python.cljc:481`),\n   322\tand the codec faithfully persists every such mark (`v2.cljc:412`). The\n   323\tboundary projection drops those marks: a mark no evaluator can\n   324\tobserve is not part of the program's syntax as the evaluators read it. The\n   325\tmark on an `:if`'s branches is carried by the branch `:application` nodes\n   326\tthemselves, which is how `yin.vm.semantic.md` §5.3's \"branches inherit it\"\n   327\tis realized.\n   328\t\n   329\t### 2.4 Saturation\n   330\t\n   331\tA row carries every default the loader would otherwise apply, so a\n   332\tprogram written with a default omitted and one written with it stated are\n   333\tone value with one address (UCF §7.3.2, *Saturation*). Saturation is a\n   334\trule of the projection boundary: the map→row direction materializes\n   335\tdefaults, the row→map direction never re-derives or re-defaults anything,\n   336\tand a row with a nil in a saturated slot is invalid, not defaulted.\n   337\t\n   338\t+----------------------------------------------------+-------------------------------------+-----------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n   339\t| Slot                                               | Default written                     | Source of the default today                                                 |\n   340\t+====================================================+=====================================+=============================================================================+\n   341\t| `:vm/gensym` prefix                                | `\"id\"`                              | walker `ast_walker.cljc:395`, codec `v2.cljc:444`, loader                   |\n   342\t|                                                    |                                     | `semantic.cljc:576`                                                         |\n   343\t+----------------------------------------------------+-------------------------------------+-----------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n   344\t| `:stream/make` buffer                              | `vm/default-stream-capacity` = 1024 | walker `ast_walker.cljc:439-440`, codec `v2.cljc:453`, loader               |\n   345\t|                                                    |                                     | `semantic.cljc:580-581`                                                     |\n   346\t+----------------------------------------------------+-------------------------------------+-----------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n   347\t| `:application` tail?                               | `false`                             | codec emits only `true` (`v2.cljc:412`); the linearizer reads `(boolean …)` |\n   348\t|                                                    |                                     | (`linearize.cljc:107-108`)                                                  |\n   349\t+----------------------------------------------------+-------------------------------------+-----------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n   350\t| `:application` / `:dao.stream.apply/call` operands | `[]`                                | walker `ast_walker.cljc:383`                                                |\n   351\t+----------------------------------------------------+-------------------------------------+-----------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n   352\t\n   353\t### 2.5 Exclusions, occurrences, and side tables\n   354\t\n   355\tExclusion is a rule of the projection boundary, the same rule §2.4 states\n   356\tfor saturation: these facts are stripped map→rows and never re-derived\n   357\trows→map. The map AST a frontend emits carries them; no row does:\n   358\t\n   359\t- **Source positions**, **parameter/variable names**, and any frontend metadata (`:yang/*` keys, Clojure\n   360\t  reader metadata on symbols). The boundary projection retains named variables in the canonical tuples. Secondary side tables are still emitted for source position tracking, but the bytecode itself preserves semantics.\n   361\t- **`:macro?`** and `:phase-policy`. `yang.clojure` sets them on defmacro\n   362\t  lambdas (`clojure.cljc:436-438`) and the codec persists `:yin/macro?`\n   363\t  (`v2.cljc:419-420`, schema `v2.cljc:312`). No evaluator reads them\n   364\t  (`ast_walker.cljc:20-23`). The expander needs the fact that a particular\n   365\t  definition is a macro; that fact travels beside the tree as an\n   366\t  occurrence-bound declaration (§8.5), not as a slot.\n   367\t- **`:eid`** (§4.4).\n   368\t- **`:yin/root`.** A tree is its own root; the batch or the ref that names\n   369\t  the tree names the root. No root fact exists in a row — the root row's\n   370\t  id is the root fact (§4.1).\n   371\t- **`t`, `m`, provenance.** Every provenance fact the codec or linearizer\n   372\t  writes today (`:yin.code/source`, `:yin.code/derived-from`) becomes a\n   373\t  ledger record (§8) or a side-table entry.\n   374\t\n   375\t**Occurrences.** Content identity names *what*; three further things\n   376\tname *where*, and each is a distinct coordinate:\n   377\t\n   378\t- A **structural path** names a place inside one tree: a vector of slot\n   379\t  indices from the root, with an index into a `nodes` slot written as a\n   380\t  pair `[slot i]`. `[]` is the root; `[2 [1 0]]` is the first operand of\n   381\t  the root's second slot. Slot indices are the row's positions, which the\n   382\t  §2.3 dictionary fixes against the map's key order, so a path reads\n   383\t  identically on both sides of the round trip. A path is a pure function\n   384\t  of the tree, stable across media and hosts. `[root-address path]`\n   385\t  separates two identical subtrees at two places in one tree, and nothing\n   386\t  more.\n   387\t- A **source occurrence** names one admission of one tree: `[medium batch\n   388\t  j]`, the program medium's identity, the batch coordinate on it (the\n   389\t  `:yin/source-batch` of `yin.vm.macro.md` §4.1), and the **member index**\n   390\t  `j` of the tree within the batch (§8.5's ordered batch carries several\n   391\t  trees). Two identical trees parsed from two files, admitted in two\n   392\t  batches, or sitting at two indices of one batch have one root address\n   393\t  and two source occurrences. A root address alone never identifies a\n   394\t  source, and neither does a batch without its member index.\n   395\t- A **producing event** names one generated tree: the `:expand` event\n   396\t  (§8.4) whose output it is. Two expansion attempts with identical output\n   397\t  have one output address and two producing events. An event has two\n   398\t  identities, used in two places: a **log-local entity id**, which only a\n   399\t  declared reference attribute on the same log may name, and the\n   400\t  **address of its expansion record** (§8.2), which is portable.\n   401\t\n   402\tAn **occurrence key** is therefore `[origin root-address path]`, where\n   403\t`origin` is `[:source medium batch j]` for admitted syntax and\n   404\t`[:expansion record-address]` for generated syntax. Every side table and\n   405\tevery provenance link that must distinguish places, sources, or attempts\n   406\tis keyed by an occurrence key, never by an address alone, and the member\n   407\tindex travels in the origin uniformly: declarations, source positions,\n   408\tinstruction provenance, and initial expansion events all carry the same\n   409\t`[:source medium batch j]`. Joining a position table to a batch table on\n   410\troot address is not a substitute: it yields every batch-position\n   411\tcombination, which is exactly the ambiguity the origin coordinate\n   412\tremoves. A bare log-local event number never appears inside an occurrence\n   413\tkey, because a key travels and the number does not (§8.4).\n   414\t\n   415\t+------------------------+------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+----------------------+\n   416\t| Side table             | Row                                                                                                                    | Keeper               |\n   417\t+========================+========================================================================================================================+======================+\n   418\t| source positions       | `[[:source medium batch j] root-address path file line col]`                                                           | the frontend that    |\n   419\t|                        |                                                                                                                        | parsed the file      |\n   420\t+------------------------+------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+----------------------+\n   421\t| frontend metadata      | `[origin root-address path key value]`                                                                                 | the frontend         |\n   422\t+------------------------+------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+----------------------+\n   423\t| variable names         | `[origin root-address path i name]`: the `i`-th parameter of the `:lambda` at `path` was written `name`; a `:variable` row's display name is the join through its binding lambda's path and index | the frontend         |\n   424\t+------------------------+------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+----------------------+\n   425\t| instruction provenance | `[segment-address pc origin root-address path]`                                                                        | the lowering (§5.3)  |\n   426\t+------------------------+------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+----------------------+\n   427\t| macro declarations     | `[[:source medium batch j] path]`: the definition at `path` of the batch's `j`-th tree is a macro definition; inside   | the frontend,        |\n   428\t|                        | the batch envelope this is written `[j path :yin.macro/definition]` (§8.5)                                             | consumed by the      |\n   429\t|                        |                                                                                                                        | expander             |\n   430\t+------------------------+------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+----------------------+\n   431\t| harvest catalogue      | `[[:source medium batch] h j path]`: occurrence `[j path]` belongs to the `h`-th **original definition** in the        | the frontend or the  |\n   432\t|                        | batch's admission order, plain or macro; one original definition shared at several paths has several rows with one     | §9.1 adapter,        |\n   433\t|                        | `h`. The macro-only declaration ordinal `k` is derived from this table and the declarations, not stored (§8.5)         | consumed by the      |\n   434\t|                        |                                                                                                                        | expander             |\n   435\t+------------------------+------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+----------------------+\n   436\t\n   437\t### 2.6 A walker-local note on runtime mutation\n   438\t\n   439\tThe frame re-scheme this section once carried is withdrawn as a design\n   440\trequirement (owner ruling, 2026-09-15): the walker stays on map ASTs, and\n   441\truntime `assoc` into a map node is legal, because the held node is the\n   442\tmachine's ephemeral image of the code, not a canonical value (§2.1), and\n   443\tnothing runtime-`assoc`ed is ever projected back to rows. The reasoning\n   444\tworth keeping, as a note and not a rule: today's cold arms `assoc`\n   445\t`:operator-evaluated?`/`:fn` (`ast_walker.cljc:213-217`) and `:evaluated`\n   446\t(`:226-237`), and the hot arms the same (`:532-541`, `:576-580`);\n   447\t`:operator-evaluated?` is redundant with the frame type, and a walker that\n   448\twanted the node read-only could keep every mutable field a sibling key of\n   449\tthe frame map — an implementation choice, available at any time, required\n   450\tby nothing here. Frame continuations remain runtime state, not syntax, as\n   451\tdo closures (`ast_walker.cljc:364-370`) and reified continuations\n   452\t(`ast_walker.cljc:422-427`). When continuation frames are themselves serialized or projected to datoms, the runtime keys the walker assoc'd into nodes must be stripped at that boundary.\n   453\t\n   454\t---\n   455\t\n   456\t## §3 The two boundary calls\n   457\t\n   458\t### 3.1 `:vm/store-update` is excluded from the canonical grammar\n   459\t\n   460\tThe node carries a host function in its `:fn` field and applies it inside\n   461\tthe syntax (`ast_walker.cljc:410-415`). It is excluded on two grounds, and\n   462\tthey are sufficient: canonical content admits no host value (§2.2,\n   463\t`data`), and the node is absent from every persistent and portable form\n   464\talready, since the codec has no arm and throws on it (`v2.cljc:413-478`),\n   465\tthe linearizer lists it as unsupported (`linearize.cljc:19-22`), the\n   466\tsemantic VM has no opcode for it, and no test constructs one (a search of\n   467\t`test/` for the node type on 2026-09-14 found none). Its one reachable\n   468\tpath is an in-process, hand-built map AST handed straight to the walker;\n   469\tno row can carry it (§2.2, `data`), so it never crosses the boundary in\n   470\teither direction.\n   471\t\n   472\tThe orchestrator's alternative, a `:fn` slot restricted to a named\n   473\tprimitive symbol resolved and profile-checked per UCF §7.5.2, is not\n   474\tadopted: it would re-admit a node that no persistent form carries, for a\n   475\tconvenience no corpus program uses.\n   476\t\n   477\tA program that needs the behaviour writes it as an application of the\n   478\t`yin/def` primitive (`v2.cljc:128`) over a `:vm/store-get` operand:\n   479\t`(yin/def k (f (vm/store-get k) args…))`. That rewrite is equivalent to\n   480\tthe arm **only under stated conditions**, and this document does not claim\n   481\tit as a general semantics-preserving migration:\n   482\t\n   483\t- `yin/def` and `f` resolve through `resolve-var`'s precedence, store\n   484\t  → primitives → modules (`src/cljc/yin/vm/engine.cljc:46-58`), so the\n   485\t  rewrite is equivalent only when the frontend resolves both `yin/def` and `f` as `:global` at the site (and not as `:variable` indices);\n   486\t- the arm stores whatever `(apply f current args)` returns, as data\n   487\t  (`ast_walker.cljc:410-415`), while an application interprets an\n   488\t  effect-shaped return (`ast_walker.cljc:184-188`,\n   489\t  `semantic.cljc:206-220`). An effect descriptor is itself plain data:\n   490\t  `module/effect?` recognizes any map carrying `:effect`\n   491\t  (`src/cljc/yin/vm/module.cljc:77-79`), and\n   492\t  `{:effect :vm/store-put :key :x :val 4}` passes `plain-data?`. So the\n   493\t  rewrite is equivalent only when `f` returns plain data **that is not an\n   494\t  effect descriptor under the applicable execution contract**; a result\n   495\t  the contract would interpret is stored by the arm and executed by the\n   496\t  rewrite.\n   497\t\n   498\t### 3.2 `:stream/close` exists, in both codec and walker\n   499\t\n   500\tOne grammar truth: the instruction is real. The codec writes it\n   501\t(`v2.cljc:465-467`) and reads it back (`v2.cljc:549-550`), the linearizer\n   502\tlowers it (`linearize.cljc:135-136`), `yin.vm.code/mnemonics` admits\n   503\t`:stream-close` (`src/cljc/yin/vm/code.cljc:12-16`), the semantic VM\n   504\tdecodes it (`semantic.cljc:585`) and executes it in its hot loop\n   505\t(`semantic.cljc:397-399`), and the engine handles the effect\n   506\t(`engine.cljc:480-482`). Only the walker lacks the arm\n   507\t(`ast_walker.cljc:359-469`).\n   508\t\n   509\tThe walker gains it: evaluate `source`, then raise `{:effect :stream/close\n   510\t:stream ref}` through `engine/handle-effect`, through an\n   511\t`:eval-stream-close-source` frame shaped exactly as\n   512\t`:eval-stream-cursor-source` (`ast_walker.cljc:319-326`). Removing the\n   513\tinstruction instead would delete working behaviour from the default\n   514\tevaluator to match the non-default one; the parity suite is the reason the\n   515\ttwo evaluators share one corpus.\n   516\t\n   517\t---\n   518\t\n   519\t## §4 Addressing\n   520\t\n   521\t### 4.1 The grain\n   522\t\n   523\tThe address of a tree is the id of its root row. A row's id is\n   524\t`(dao.jing/segment-key body)` (`jing.cljc:217-225`) over the row's body —\n   525\tthe tag, the data slots unchanged, the children as ids (§2.1) — so a\n   526\tparent's id commits to its children's ids and every node is addressed by\n   527\tconstruction; nothing coarser is hashed and no node needs a finer grain.\n   528\tA root id is still a whole-tree address: following child ids from the\n   529\troot row recovers the entire tree, and every named payoff below names\n   530\texactly this id.\n   531\t\n   532\t+-----------------------------------------------+------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n   533\t| Payoff                                        | How it works at the root-row grain                                                                                     |\n   534\t+===============================================+========================================================================================================================+\n   535\t| `:yin.code/derived-from` as a content address | a derivation record naming the tree address as input and the segment address as output (§8.2) replaces the entity ref  |\n   536\t|                                               | of `v2.cljc:327` and `linearize.cljc:212`                                                                              |\n   537\t+-----------------------------------------------+------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n   538\t| the macro ledger                              | an expansion event naming input, output, and macro addresses (§8.4): expansion is a function from address to address,  |\n   539\t|                                               | and each attempt is its own event                                                                                      |\n   540\t+-----------------------------------------------+------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n   541\t| module manifests pinning AST content          | a manifest names the tree addresses it exports; equal content is equal address across hosts                            |\n   542\t+-----------------------------------------------+------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n   543\t| lowering as an address-to-address function    | `lower : tree → instruction vector`, and each application of it is one derivation record (§5.2)                        |\n   544\t+-----------------------------------------------+------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n   545\t\n   546\tThe address is deterministic and operand-order-sensitive: `(add 1 2)` and\n   547\t`(add 2 1)` address differently, because `order-normalize` sorts maps and\n   548\tsets but leaves vectors in place (`jing.cljc:45-64`). Literal maps and sets\n   549\tinside a `data` slot hash order-insensitively, which is correct: they are\n   550\tvalues.\n   551\t\n   552\t### 4.2 The transitional encoder is inherited, and every identity use is blocked on it\n   553\t\n   554\t`segment-key` hashes an order-normalized `pr-str` (`jing.cljc:67-71`,\n   555\t`:203-214`). `order-normalize` coerces **every** sequential value to a\n   556\tvector (`jing.cljc:63`), and `pr-str` prints no metadata. The encoder is\n   557\ttherefore not injective over the `data` domain, and the collisions are not\n   558\tconfined to one collection type:\n   559\t\n   560\t+--------------------------------------------------------+------------------------------------+--------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n   561\t| Two different values                                   | One address today                  | Why it matters                                                           |\n   562\t+========================================================+====================================+==========================================================================+\n   563\t| `[:literal [1 2]]` and `[:literal (1 2)]`              | lists become vectors               | `conj` appends to one and prepends to the other                          |\n   564\t+--------------------------------------------------------+------------------------------------+--------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n   565\t| `[:literal [1 2]]` and `[:literal (seq [1 2])]`        | a seq is sequential but not a list | the same, and a list reject-rule does not see it                         |\n   566\t+--------------------------------------------------------+------------------------------------+--------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n   567\t| `[:literal ^{:a 1} [1 2]]` and `[:literal ^{:a 2} [1   | metadata is dropped by `pr-str`    | `data` admits plain metadata (`linearize.cljc:58`) and programs may read |\n   568\t| 2]]`                                                   |                                    | it                                                                       |\n   569\t+--------------------------------------------------------+------------------------------------+--------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n   570\t\n   571\tThis is `dao.jing.md`'s first open item (*Canonical encoding*), inherited\n   572\tunchanged, and it is an acceptance blocker of this design (§10.1).\n   573\t\n   574\t**Every identity and deduplication use is blocked until the encoding is\n   575\tfixed.** No restricted value domain is claimed, and none is admitted: a\n   576\treject-list over collection types does not make address-based identity\n   577\tsafe, because the admitted domain is open (`plain-data?` admits every\n   578\t`coll?`, `linearize.cljc:56`) and the encoder's losses are structural. The\n   579\tblock applies uniformly to every addressed value and every use of an\n   580\taddress as identity: `data` slots of trees, `:const` and `:store-put`\n   581\toperands of vectors, ledger-record maps (§8.2), flat-projection row\n   582\tcollapse (§6.1), DAG sharing (§4.4), process-local address-keyed caches,\n   583\t`dao.jing` reads that resolve an address to a literal value, and every\n   584\tledger row that names an address. Process lifetime is irrelevant. Until\n   585\tthe block lifts, addresses may be *computed* and *carried* for the\n   586\tconformance work of §7.2, but nothing may treat two equal addresses as\n   587\tone value.\n   588\t\n   589\tThe three rows above are part of the **encoder conformance obligation**\n   590\tthat lifts the block: the pinned encoding must address each pair\n   591\tdistinctly, and the test that proves it is part of `dao.jing`'s\n   592\tacceptance, not this document's. When it lands, every address minted under\n   593\tthis design changes with every other `dao.jing` address, the dependency\n   594\tUCF §7.3.2 already accepts.\n   595\t\n   596\t### 4.3 Every node is addressed by construction\n   597\t\n   598\tPer-node addressing is not a projection consequence and no longer a\n   599\tfollow-up: a row's id is its node's address by construction (§2.1), so\n   600\tevery node of every tree is named, shareable, and queryable the moment it\n   601\tis projected. The round-7 question — a `:lambda` whose body is\n   602\t*materialized separately*, referred to by address from the canonical form\n   603\tof the enclosing tree, pinned by manifests on its own — is the ordinary\n   604\tshape of the stored row DAG, not a canonical-form extension: every child\n   605\tslot already refers by address, and a manifest may name any row's id. What\n   606\tremains of it is a lowering question — one segment per lambda rather than\n   607\tout-of-line bodies in one segment (`linearize.cljc:151-158`) — which is a\n   608\t§5.2.1 profile concern, changes no row address, and is not open here.\n   609\t\n   610\t### 4.4 Sharing by content address replaces `:eid`; occurrences stay outside\n   611\t\n   612\tToday a shared subtree is emitted once and referenced by a pre-assigned\n   613\tentity id: `yang.clojure` allocates one for each defmacro lambda\n   614\t(`clojure.cljc:418-437`) and the codec deduplicates on it\n   615\t(`v2.cljc:394-411`). Its purpose is that the definition operand and each\n   616\tcall-site operator resolve to one entity so provenance can point at it.\n   617\t\n   618\tContent addressing gives that for free and in every case, not only the one\n   619\t`yang.clojure` remembers to mark: two occurrences of one subtree have one\n   620\taddress by construction and one row in the stored set, and a query \"which\n   621\tsites reference this lambda's content\" is a join on its address (§6.4).\n   622\tBecause names are excluded from the canonical tuple (§2.5), this structural\n   623\tsharing is alpha-equivalent sharing: structurally identical functions up to\n   624\tvariable renaming project to the identical row. The row set holds each distinct\n   625\tsubtree once, and parent slots point at it — while identity stays per row.\n   626\t**However, this structural collapse is strictly gated on the §4.2 encoder fix.** Because the current encoder is non-injective, structurally distinct values that hash equal would silently merge into one row, corrupting the AST. Until the encoder is fixed, the row form as a whole is gated.\n   627\t`:eid` is dropped from the frontends and the codec.\n   628\t\n   629\tWhat `:eid` also did, and content addresses do not, is name a *place*.\n   630\t\"This call site\" is an occurrence key `[origin root-address path]` (§2.5),\n   631\tnot the site's content address; a query that needs distinct call sites\n   632\tjoins the occurrence relation, not subtree addresses, and a provenance\n   633\tlink that must survive a second identical site, a second identical file,\n   634\tor a second identical expansion records the occurrence key. Content\n   635\taddresses serve sharing and cross-media correspondence of content;\n   636\toccurrence keys serve identity of places, sources, and attempts.\n   637\t\n   638\t---\n   639\t\n   640\t## §5 The segment level\n   641\t\n   642\t### 5.1 The instruction vector is UCF §7.3.2\n   643\t\n   644\tThe segment's canonical form is the positional instruction tuple vector of\n   645\tUCF §7.3.2: one tuple per instruction, pc is the index, the\n   646\t`yin.vm.semantic.md` §2.4 table fixes each mnemonic's arity and operand\n   647\tkinds, defaults are saturated, refs are resolved pcs, and the header folds\n   648\taway. Its address is `(dao.jing/segment-key vector)`, the value\n   649\t`yin.vm.semantic.md` §2.2 already types `:yin.code/hash` as. The two\n   650\tlevels are uniform under this design: an AST row (§2.1) and an instruction\n   651\ttuple are both flat positional tuples, canonical and content-addressed,\n   652\tand the instruction vector was already this shape before the flat-row\n   653\truling clarified the AST level. To perfectly preserve program semantics across boundaries, both the Universal Map AST and the Semantic Tuples strictly retain named variables. Therefore, `\"v2\"` everywhere in this document means the Named Variable grammar. This design otherwise adds\n   654\tnothing to that form and restates none of it.\n   655\t\n   656\t### 5.2 The hash chain is realized by derivation records\n   657\t\n   658\t```\n   659\ttree-address ──lower under lowering profile L, targeting execution contract C──▶ segment-address\n   660\t```\n   661\t\n   662\tNeither value carries the other's address: the tree is the input, the\n   663\tvector excludes `:yin.code/derived-from` (UCF §7.3.2, *The header folds\n   664\taway*). The link is a **derivation record**, itself content-addressed\n   665\t(§8.2):\n   666\t\n   667\t```clojure\n   668\t{:yin.ledger/op       :derive\n   669\t :yin.ledger/input    <tree-address>\n   670\t :yin.ledger/output   <segment-address>\n   671\t :yin.ledger/function :yin.vm/lower\n   672\t :yin.ledger/profile  {:yin.lower/profile \"ast-v1\"          ; the lowering profile, §5.2.1\n   673\t                       :yin.code/contract \"v2\"              ; the UCF §7.3.3 execution contract it targets\n   674\t                       :yin.k/version     0}}\n   675\t```\n   676\t\n   677\tIts address is published in the ledger (§8.3). The chain\n   678\t`record → input, record → output` is what \"the hash chain is the ledger\"\n   679\tmeans under this design: the chain lives in ledger records and refs, not\n   680\tin the code content, which rightly excludes it.\n   681\t\n   682\t#### 5.2.1 The lowering profile\n   683\t\n   684\tUCF §7.3.3's execution-contract stamp versions the instruction grammar,\n   685\tthe transitions, resolution precedence, effect outcomes, and scheduling.\n   686\tIts declared scope does not include the AST-to-instruction algorithm, and\n   687\ttwo deterministic lowerers can target one execution contract and emit two\n   688\tdifferent, equally valid vectors. A derivation is therefore verifiable\n   689\tonly against a **lowering profile**, a published revision that pins:\n   690\t\n   691\t+---------------------------------------+--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n   692\t| Pinned by the profile                 | Reference value for `\"ast-v1\"`                                                                                                 |\n   693\t+=======================================+================================================================================================================================+\n   694\t| input grammar                         | the §2 tuple grammar, by revision of this document                                                                             |\n   695\t+---------------------------------------+--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n   696\t| normalization applied before emission | saturation (§2.4), tail marks on `:application` only (§2.3), nothing else                                                      |\n   697\t+---------------------------------------+--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n   698\t| emission rules                        | the §5.3 flattening table of `yin.vm.semantic.md`, in the walker's evaluation order (operator, then operands left to right)    |\n   699\t+---------------------------------------+--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n   700\t| label and body order                  | labels numbered in emission order (`linearize.cljc:76-77`); bodies emitted after the main sequence, in the order lambdas were  |\n   701\t|                                       | encountered, including lambdas found inside bodies (`linearize.cljc:151-158`)                                                  |\n   702\t+---------------------------------------+--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n   703\t| terminators                           | main sequence ends in `:halt`, every body in `:return` (`linearize.cljc:150`, `:157`)                                          |\n   704\t+---------------------------------------+--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n   705\t| target execution contract             | the UCF §7.3.3 stamp the emitted grammar must satisfy                                                                          |\n   706\t+---------------------------------------+--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n   707\t\n   708\tThe profile is a dedicated stamp, not an extension of the execution\n   709\tcontract: an execution contract may be targeted by several profiles, and a\n   710\tprofile is revised whenever any pinned rule changes, whether or not the\n   711\texecution contract does. A lowering that introduces any host-dependent\n   712\tchoice violates its profile and is a defect. The profile is the structured\n   713\tmap above, carried **exactly** in the record and in the event projection\n   714\t(§8.2); no string form of it is defined and none is used.\n   715\t\n   716\t#### 5.2.2 Verification, in two separate steps\n   717\t\n   718\tA consumer holding a tree, a vector, and a derivation record verifies two\n   719\tdifferent things and reports them separately:\n   720\t\n   721\t1. **Content integrity.** The consumer verifies the claimed root ID against the root row and verifies each required row as `row.id == segment-key(row-body)`, recursively validating the reachable closure before reconstructing the semantic map. The instruction vector is verified similarly. Failure is\n   722\t   `:yin.k/hash-mismatch` naming the value. This is UCF §7.3.4's check and\n   723\t   says nothing about the derivation.\n   724\t2. **Derivation.** If, and only if, the consumer implements the record's\n   725\t   `:yin.lower/profile` exactly, it lowers the reconstructed map under that profile and verifies the resulting instruction-vector address against `:yin.ledger/output`. Equality\n   726\t   verifies the asserted derivation; inequality is `:yin.k/derivation-\n   727\t   mismatch`, a defective or dishonest lowering, never repaired silently. A\n   728\t   consumer implementing a different profile does not recompute: it reports\n   729\t   `:yin.k/profile-mismatch` naming both profiles, the same outcome UCF\n   730\t   §7.3.3 gives a different execution contract, and it may still *load* the\n   731\t   output vector if the vector's own execution contract is one it\n   732\t   implements, because content integrity (step 1) is independent of who\n   733\t   lowered it.\n   734\t\n   735\tTwo trees that lower to one vector under one profile yield two records\n   736\twith one output; one tree lowered under two profiles yields two records\n   737\twith two outputs, and neither is corruption.\n   738\t\n   739\t### 5.3 Instruction provenance\n   740\t\n   741\t`:yin.code/source` (`v2.cljc:326`, `linearize.cljc:225`) leaves the\n   742\tinstruction: UCF §7.3.2 excludes it from the vector, and the tuple form has\n   743\tnowhere else to put it. It becomes the side table `[segment-address pc\n   744\torigin root-address path]` of §2.5, emitted by the lowering beside the\n   745\tvector and keyed by the full occurrence key, so that two identical call\n   746\tsites, two identical files, two identical trees at two batch indices, or\n   747\ttwo expansion attempts map to distinct instruction ranges. The `origin`,\n   748\tincluding the member index `j` for admitted syntax, is an input to the\n   749\tlowering, supplied by the composition that admitted or generated the\n   750\ttree; the lowering never infers it. Rendering an instruction range back to\n   751\tsource is a join over that table and the source-position table on the\n   752\toccurrence key, which is the query `yin.vm.semantic.md` §5.3 says it is;\n   753\tfor generated code the join goes through the producing event's record\n   754\taddress to its call occurrence (§8.4).\n   755\t\n   756\t---\n   757\t\n   758\t## §6 Querying\n   759\t\n   760\t### 6.1 The row relation and the two directions around it\n   761\t\n   762\tThe flat rows are the canonical stored form (§2.1), and `q` runs over\n   763\tthem directly — ruling 3 of 2026-09-14 was verified live over exactly\n   764\tthis shape. A stored set holds one row per distinct subtree: identical\n   765\tsubtrees project to the identical row, so the set is the tree's DAG\n   766\t(§4.4). Because a tag has one arity in the tree, it has one arity in the\n   767\trelation (tag arity plus one for the id). Rows of one tag are\n   768\thomogeneous, so a where-pattern for a tag has exactly that tag's arity\n   769\tand no padding. Rows from many trees union into one relation without\n   770\tcollision, because ids are addresses. An occurrence count is a count\n   771\tover parent slots or over the occurrence relation below, never over\n   772\trows.\n   773\t\n   774\t**The Dedicated AST Indexer:** This row relation (called `$ast` in queries) is maintained by the dedicated AST indexer (§1). The indexer is a `dao.stream` observer peer to the evaluators (§7.1); its input is the row batches of §7.1; its output is this row relation plus the occurrence relation below; it is distinct from `dao.space.index`, which indexes datoms only (§6.5); it is a projection keeper per §1's Query layer, so discarding it loses nothing.\n   775\t\n   776\t`map → rows` is the **codec boundary projection** (`yin.vm/ast->semantic-bytecode`): strip the §2.5\n   777\texclusions, saturate per §2.4, positionalize per the §2.3 dictionary,\n   778\tmerkle per §4.1. It is the standing contract of the Encoder Observer, not a\n   779\tmigration device (§9.1).\n   780\t\n   781\t`rows → map` is **load-time reconstruction** (§7.1): validate the rows\n   782\t(§7.4), then rebuild the map AST through the ids. The precedent is\n   783\t`datoms->ast` (`src/cljc/yin/vm.cljc:494-559`), which already\n   784\trebuilds map ASTs from flat rows through entity ids — a `get-attr` per\n   785\tfield over an entity index, recursion through child ids; the loader is\n   786\tits successor with content addresses in place of allocated ids.\n   787\t\n   788\tDeduplication — treating two equal ids as one value — is an identity use\n   789\tand is blocked by §4.2 until the encoding is fixed; until then a stored\n   790\tset may not be collapsed on id equality, and the occurrence relation\n   791\tmust be carried beside the rows wherever places, not contents, are asked\n   792\tabout.\n   793\t\n   794\tA second relation, `occurrences : tree → #{[root-address path\n   795\tnode-address]}`, one row per place, is the structural half of the join\n   796\tbetween rows and the occurrence-keyed side tables of §2.5. It is a pure\n   797\tfunction of the tree, computable from either side of the round trip, so\n   798\tit carries no origin; a query fixes the origin from the composition's\n   799\tside (which batch, which event) and joins on `[root-address path]`\n   800\twithin that origin. It is computed by the same walk that computes the\n   801\tprojection.\n   802\t\n   803\t### 6.2 Segment rows\n   804\t\n   805\t`project-segment : vector → [[pc tag & ops] …]`, the instruction vector with\n   806\tpc prepended. This relation is per segment: pcs collide across segments,\n   807\tand source scope belongs to the interpreter, never to a tuple slot\n   808\t(`datom.world.md:34`). A query over several segments takes them as\n   809\tseparate sources (`:in $ $2 …`) or uses the **segment-qualified form**\n   810\t`[segment-address pc tag & ops]`, which adds one to every tag's arity\n   811\tuniformly and keeps per-tag arity fixed. Bare-pc rows from different\n   812\tsegments are never unioned into one relation.\n   813\t\n   814\t### 6.3 `q` over both, and the mechanics it rests on\n   815\t\n   816\t`q` accepts these relations through `query/relation`\n   817\t(`src/cljc/dao/space/query.cljc:153-158`), whose contents are an arbitrary\n   818\tmixed-dimensional tuple collection. A pattern clause is exact-arity\n   819\tpositional unification: `eval-pattern-clause` (`query.cljc:894-907`) parses\n   820\tthe pattern (`query.cljc:419-436`), and on the general path unifies every\n   821\trow through `unify-slots` (`query.cljc:799-809`), which first requires\n   822\t`tuple-shape-matches?` (`query.cljc:439-441`) and then unifies slot by\n   823\tslot. A final `& ?tail` binds the remaining slots as a vector and `& _`\n   824\tignores them (`query.cljc:431-436`, `:807-808`). The 3-slot EAV fast path\n   825\t(`query.cljc:899`) engages only when the source carries a fact index, which\n   826\t`fact-relation` (`query.cljc:172-177`) and the datom views supply; the code\n   827\trelations never do, so every code pattern takes the general path by\n   828\tconstruction.\n   829\t\n   830\tVerified shapes, each of which is a rule a query author may rely on:\n   831\t\n   832\t- **Per-tag selection.** `[?id :variable ?name]` matches only arity-3 rows.\n   833\t- **Joins across arities.** `[?site :application ?op _ _] [?op :global\n   834\t  ?f]` joins an arity-5 row to an arity-3 row on the address.\n   835\t- **Operand membership** goes through a predicate: `[(member? ?operands\n   836\t  ?x)]` with `member?` supplied under `:fns`; predicate arguments must\n   837\t  already be bound.\n   838\t- **Ledger as-of** (§8.6) and **ref-to-code joins** (§6.4) run in one\n   839\t  query, the ref pattern on the fast path and the code pattern on the\n   840\t  general path.\n   841\t\n   842\tTwo gotchas are rules:\n   843\t\n   844\t- `match` (`query.cljc:520-528`) is constant-or-wildcard selection:\n   845\t  `slots-match?` (`query.cljc:444-449`) treats only `_`, nil, and the\n   846\t  internal free marker as wildcards (`query.cljc:409-411`), so a `?var`\n   847\t  written in a `match` pattern is compared as a constant and binds nothing.\n   848\t  Use `q` to bind.\n   849\t- `:fns` keys are quoted symbols: `eval-fn-clause` looks the clause head up\n   850\t  as a symbol (`query.cljc:1099`), and the builtins merge under the same\n   851\t  keys (`query.cljc:1436-1438`). `{:fns {'member? f}}`, as\n   852\t  `test/dao/space/stigmergy_test.clj:228` writes it.\n   853\t\n   854\t### 6.4 Refs joined by address\n   855\t\n   856\tA ref is a datom whose `v` is an address (§8). A name is an entity carrying\n   857\t`:yin/name`; joining refs to code is a join on the address value:\n   858\t\n   859\t```clojure\n   860\t;; every call site, in the tree currently named `my.ns/f`, whose operator is `+`\n   861\t[:find ?root ?path\n   862\t :in $refs $code $occ\n   863\t :where [$refs ?e :yin/name my.ns/f]          ; bind the name entity\n   864\t        [$refs ?e :yin/code ?root]            ; 3-slot, fast path, current view\n   865\t        [$code ?site :application ?op _ _]    ; general path\n   866\t        [$code ?op :global +]\n   867\t        [$occ ?root ?path ?site]]             ; every place that content occurs in this tree\n   868\t```\n   869\t\n   870\t`$refs` is a `current` view (§8.6), so the name resolves to its current\n   871\taddress. The result names places, not contents: the occurrence relation\n   872\tturns one shared site row into one result per place.\n   873\t\n   874\t### 6.5 What goes to the datom projection instead\n   875\t\n   876\tA tree also projects to datoms, `[e a v t m]` with tempids minted in\n   877\tpreorder and one extra fact `[e :yin/address addr]` per node so the\n   878\tprojection joins back to refs. This is the successor of `ast->datoms`\n   879\t(`v2.cljc:372-482`) and is the *projection path* of §7.1. It exists for\n   880\tthree queries the flat rows deliberately do not serve:\n   881\t\n   882\t+----------------------------------------------------------------------+-------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n   883\t| Query                                                                | Why the datom projection                                                                        |\n   884\t+======================================================================+=================================================================================================+\n   885\t| attribute-dimension queries with the attribute unbound, `[?e ?a ?v]` | a flat row has no attribute slot; \"every fact about this node\" is an EAV question               |\n   886\t+----------------------------------------------------------------------+-------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n   887\t| `pull` and `entity-attrs` navigation (`query.cljc:715`, `:1481`)     | they navigate entity → attribute → value and reverse attrs                                      |\n   888\t+----------------------------------------------------------------------+-------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n   889\t| scale indexing under covered indexes (`dao.space.index`)             | the covered-index publisher and `open-published!` are EAVT/AEVT/AVET/VAET over datoms           |\n   890\t|                                                                      | (`query.cljc:452-476`)                                                                          |\n   891\t+----------------------------------------------------------------------+-------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n   892\t\n   893\tThe datom projection never becomes an identity: its `e` values are local,\n   894\tand the only durable name in it is the `:yin/address` value.\n   895\t\n   896\t---\n   897\t\n   898\t## §7 The VM around it\n   899\t\n   900\t### 7.1 Load paths\n   901\t\n   902\tDirect row loading is primary. As `dao.stream` observers, evaluators receive batches natively from the stream. A batch on the program stream is the\n   903\tcanonical row set of one tree (AST medium) or one canonical instruction\n   904\tvector (code medium), and the evaluator's loader takes it as is:\n   905\t\n   906\t+----------------------+----------------------+--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n   907\t| Evaluator            | Loader input         | What loading does                                                                                                        |\n   908\t+======================+======================+==========================================================================================================================+\n   909\t| walker               | map AST              | natively reads map AST from stream topic without conversion — set `:program` and `:control` to the map.                    |\n   910\t|                      |                      |                                                                                                                          |\n   911\t|                      |                      |                                                                                                                          |\n   912\t+----------------------+----------------------+--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n   913\t| semantic             | instruction vector   | validate (§7.5), decode positional operands into the image (the `case` of `semantic.cljc:563-593` reading `(nth tuple i)`|\n   914\t|                      |                      | instead of attribute maps), store under `:code` with the address as the alias column UCF §7.3.4 requires                 |\n   915\t+----------------------+----------------------+--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n   916\t| semantic,            | row set              | `lower` (§5.2) then the row above; composed by the composition exactly as `linearize/ast-loader` is today                |\n   917\t| AST medium           |                      | (`linearize.cljc:269-286`, chosen in `src/cljc/yin/repl/core.cljc:63-68`)                                             |\n   918\t+----------------------+----------------------+--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n   919\t\n   920\tDatom-batch loading is the projection path: a batch of `:yin/*` datoms is\n   921\tprojected to rows by the datom→row half of the §9.1 codec pair and then\n   922\tloaded as above; a batch of `:yin.code/*` datoms is decoded to a vector\n   923\tby UCF §7.3.4's projection rule and then loaded as above. The projection\n   924\tpath exists for media that carry datoms and for the datom projection of\n   925\t§6.5; it is not a second loader and it runs the same validator (§7.2).\n   926\t\n   927\t### 7.2 Conformance: both paths, one validator, one image\n   928\t\n   929\t**This section supersedes the sentence in UCF §7.3.4 that names the\n   930\tprojection path as the reference path.** Under the owner's ruling the\n   931\tdirect path is primary and the reference; the projection path is derived\n   932\tfrom it. Everything else in §7.3.4 stands.\n   933\t\n   934\tBoth paths invoke the same validator (§7.4 for rows, §7.5 for vectors)\n   935\tbefore anything is registered, and the conformance obligation has four\n   936\tparts:\n   937\t\n   938\t1. **The round-trip law.** For every corpus program, `map → rows → map`\n   939\t   is the identity on the canonical map AST, and `rows → map → rows` is\n   940\t   the identity on the rows. This is the both-paths obligation restated\n   941\t   under the flat-row ontology: the projection is total and lossless,\n   942\t   and each direction is tested against the other over the corpus. The\n   943\t   corpus already lives as map ASTs in the test tree, which is what makes\n   944\t   this testable now.\n   945\t2. **Valid corpus, same result.** For every corpus program, loading the\n   946\t   rows directly and loading their datom projection yield the same walker\n   947\t   `:program` value; loading the instruction vector directly and loading\n   948\t   its datom projection yield the same image. Keyword store keys\n   949\t   (`parity_test.cljc:99`) and numeric store keys are part of the corpus\n   950\t   and must round-trip on both paths, so the `key` kind is exercised as\n   951\t   the `data` domain it is (§2.2), not as the two types the corpus\n   952\t   happened to use.\n   953\t3. **Malformed input, same refusal.** For a published set of malformed\n   954\t   vectors, each violating one rule of §7.5, and malformed row sets, each\n   955\t   violating one rule of §7.4, both paths refuse with the same defect\n   956\t   (rule and pc, or rule and path). A correctly hashed `[[:jump 9]]` is in\n   957\t   the set. Image equality on valid input does not establish this; the\n   958\t   malformed set does.\n   959\t4. **Several segments loaded.** Loading two or more segments through each\n   960\t   path yields the same `:code` map and the same address alias column, and\n   961\t   a second segment claiming a live local id fails identically on both.\n   962\t\n   963\tThe obligation fails exactly when the dictionary and the datom schema\n   964\thave drifted apart, which is the property that makes it the VM's own test\n   965\tof this design.\n   966\t\n   967\t### 7.3 Validation is the tuple grammar\n   968\t\n   969\tA tuple loader validates before it decodes, and validation is the §2 table\n   970\tplus the structural rules a segment needs, nothing else. Both checkers\n   971\tmirror `code/well-formed?` (`code.cljc:155-180`): each returns nil or the\n   972\tfirst defect, rules run in order, and each rule may assume the earlier ones\n   973\theld.\n   974\t\n   975\t### 7.4 Row rules\n   976\t\n   977\tDefects name a path from the root row (§2.5), or the row's own id for a\n   978\trow nothing reaches.\n   979\t\n   980\t+-------------------+---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n   981\t| Rule              | Defect when                                                                                                                                       |\n   982\t+===================+===================================================================================================================================================+\n   983\t| `:tag`            | the element after the id is not a tag of §2.3                                                                                                     |\n   984\t+-------------------+---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n   985\t| `:arity`          | the count differs from the tag's body arity plus one                                                                                              |\n   986\t+-------------------+---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n   987\t| `:slot-kind`      | a slot's value is not of the slot's kind: a `node` slot that is not an address, a `nodes` slot that is not a vector of addresses, a `data` or     |\n   988\t|                   | `key` slot failing `plain-data?`, a `bool` slot that is not `true`/`false`                                                                        |\n   989\t+-------------------+---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n   990\t| `:saturation`     | a saturated slot (§2.4) is nil                                                                                                                    |\n   991\t+-------------------+---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n   992\t| `:id-resolves`    | a child id in a `node` or `nodes` slot names no row of the loaded set                                                                             |\n   993\t+-------------------+---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n   994\t| `:acyclic`        | following child ids from a row revisits a row already on its own path                                                                             |\n   995\t+-------------------+---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n   996\t| `:root-reachable` | a row of the loaded set is not reachable from the root row                                                                                        |\n   997\t+-------------------+---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n   998\t| `:variable-bounds`| a `:variable` name must be bound by an enclosing `:lambda`'s params, else the frontend must have emitted `:global`; the defect names the path     |\n   999\t+-------------------+---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n  1000\t\n  1001\tThe three reference rules are the row counterpart of §7.5's\n  1002\t`:target-bounds`: a row's child slots are references, and they must\n  1003\tresolve within the loaded set and close under the root, as a jump target\n  1004\tmust land inside its segment. Acyclicity is also implied by the per-row\n  1005\taddress check whenever it runs — a row's id commits to its children's\n  1006\tids, so a cycle would break the hash (§4.1) — and the rule stands\n  1007\tregardless, so that a load taking ids as given still names the defect.\n  1008\t\n  1009\tThere is no encoder-domain rule: §4.2 blocks identity uses outright rather\n  1010\tthan admitting a domain.\n  1011\t\n  1012\t### 7.5 Vector rules\n  1013\t\n  1014\tDefects name a pc. Rules 1–4 of `yin.vm.semantic.md` §2.6 (one segment,\n  1015\tdense pcs, sorted by pc, refs resolve to entities) are satisfied by the\n  1016\tvector form by construction and have no rule here; rules 5 and 6 and the\n  1017\tUCF §7.3.4 checks are translated as follows.\n  1018\t\n  1019\t+------------------+-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n  1020\t| Rule             | Defect when                                                                                                                                         |\n  1021\t+==================+=====================================================================================================================================================+\n  1022\t| `:nonempty`      | the vector has no instructions                                                                                                                      |\n  1023\t+------------------+-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n  1024\t| `:mnemonic`      | the first element is not a mnemonic of `code/mnemonics` (`code.cljc:12-16`)                                                                         |\n  1025\t+------------------+-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n  1026\t| `:arity`         | the tuple's count differs from the mnemonic's arity in `yin.vm.semantic.md` §2.4 as saturated by UCF §7.3.2                                         |\n  1027\t+------------------+-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n  1028\t| `:operand-kind`  | an operand is not of its kind: `:const` and `:store-put` values or `:store-get`/`:store-put` keys fail `plain-data?` (the `key` kind of §2.2), a    |\n  1029\t|                  | a `:var` name or a `:global` name is not a symbol, a `:closure` params is not a vector of symbols, a `:ffi-call` op or `:resume` parked id is not a keyword, a `:gensym`  |\n  1030\t|                  | prefix is not a string                                                                                                                              |\n  1031\t+------------------+-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n  1032\t| `:saturation`    | a saturated operand (`:gensym` prefix, `:stream-make` buffer, `:call` tail?, `:ffi-call` argc) is nil                                               |\n  1033\t+------------------+-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n  1034\t| `:target-bounds` | a `:jump`/`:branch-false` target or a `:closure` body is not an integer in `[0, length)`                                                            |\n  1035\t+------------------+-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n  1036\t| `:terminator`    | the last instruction is not in `code/terminators` (`code.cljc:19-21`)                                                                               |\n  1037\t+------------------+-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n  1038\t| `:argc`          | a `:call`/`:ffi-call` argc is not a non-negative integer                                                                                            |\n  1039\t+------------------+-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n  1040\t\n  1041\tThere is no vector-level index-bounds rule: nesting is a property of the AST, checked by §7.4; an out-of-range `:var` at run time evaluates to `nil` (§7.7.2).\n  1042\t\n  1043\tThe address check (the vector hashes to the address it claims) is UCF\n  1044\t§7.3.4's and runs before these rules whenever an address is claimed; a\n  1045\tcorrect hash is never structural validation. The failure outcome of both\n  1046\tcheckers on the UCF lowering path is `:yin.k/undecodable` naming the pc or\n  1047\tpath, as §7.3.4 already specifies; on a local load it is a load error\n  1048\tnaming the same.\n  1049\t\n  1050\t### 7.6 The hot loop never queries\n  1051\t\n  1052\tThe walker dispatches on `(:type node)` and reads named fields of the map\n  1053\tAST it reconstructed at load; the semantic VM runs `(aget code pc)` over\n  1054\tan image decoded once at load (`yin.vm.semantic.md` §3.1). Neither touches\n  1055\ta relation, a ref, an address\n  1056\tindex, or a projection while stepping. Projection and query are load-time\n  1057\tand tooling-time operations. This is the rule that keeps\n  1058\t`yin.vm.semantic.md` §6's cost model intact under this design: queryability\n  1059\tis paid once at load, or by whoever asks, never per step.\n  1060\t\n  1061\t### 7.7 Dependency closure is Datalog over the tuples\n  1062\t\n  1063\tUCF §7.6.1 computes `:yin.k/requires` by a conservative fixed point over\n  1064\tvalues, code, names, and modules. The **code part** of that fixed point is\n  1065\tthe queries below, run over the flat rows of every reachable tree\n  1066\tand the segment-qualified rows (§6.2) of every reachable segment. The\n  1067\tvalue, name-satisfaction, and module parts remain the walk §7.6.1\n  1068\tspecifies; these queries supply their inputs and do not replace them.\n  1069\t\n  1070\tExtraction over trees (`$ast` is the union of flat rows of the reachable\n  1071\ttrees):\n  1072\t\n  1073\t```clojure\n  1074\t;; :global rows are exactly the free names; bound variables carry no name and contribute no obligation\n  1075\t[:find ?name :in $ast :where [$ast _ :global ?name]]\n  1076\t\n  1077\t;; store keys read or written by code\n  1078\t[:find ?key :in $ast :where (or [$ast _ :vm/store-get ?key]\n  1079\t                                [$ast _ :vm/store-put ?key _])]\n  1080\t\n  1081\t;; FFI ops\n  1082\t[:find ?op :in $ast :where [$ast _ :dao.stream.apply/call ?op _]]\n  1083\t\n  1084\t;; parked ids named by resume\n  1085\t[:find ?pid :in $ast :where [$ast _ :vm/resume ?pid _]]\n  1086\t\n  1087\t;; syntax that raises an effect: the tag, to be normalized by the footprint table below\n  1088\t[:find ?tag :in $ast\n  1089\t :where [$ast _ ?tag & _]\n  1090\t        [(contains? #{:stream/make :stream/put :stream/cursor :stream/next\n  1091\t                      :stream/close :dao.stream.apply/call} ?tag)]]\n  1092\t```\n  1093\t\n  1094\tExtraction over segments (`$code` is the union of segment-qualified rows):\n  1095\t\n  1096\t```clojure\n  1097\t[:find ?name :in $code :where [$code _ _ :global ?name]]\n  1098\t[:find ?key  :in $code :where (or [$code _ _ :store-get ?key]\n  1099\t                                  [$code _ _ :store-put ?key _])]\n  1100\t[:find ?op   :in $code :where [$code _ _ :ffi-call ?op _]]\n  1101\t[:find ?pid  :in $code :where [$code _ _ :resume ?pid]]\n  1102\t[:find ?mn   :in $code\n  1103\t :where [$code _ _ ?mn & _]\n  1104\t        [(contains? #{:stream-make :stream-put :stream-cursor :stream-next\n  1105\t                      :stream-close :ffi-call} ?mn)]]\n  1106\t```\n  1107\t\n  1108\tStore keys are extracted as values of the `key` kind (§2.2), whatever\n  1109\ttheir type, and are compared with the store slice's keys by value.\n  1110\t\n  1111\t#### 7.7.1 Effect normalization: the footprint table\n  1112\t\n  1113\tSyntax names effects by tag, segments by mnemonic, and profiles and\n  1114\t`:yin.k/requires` by **effect identifier** (`:stream/put`, UCF §7.6.1's\n  1115\t`:yin.k/effects`). The reference machine performs the mnemonic-to-effect\n  1116\tmapping in its hot loop (`semantic.cljc:360-403`: `:stream-make` raises\n  1117\t`{:effect :stream/make}`, `:stream-put` raises `{:effect :stream/put}`, and\n  1118\tso on; `:ffi-call` goes through the FFI pair, `semantic.cljc:405-420`).\n  1119\tThat mapping is lifted out of the machine into a **footprint table**,\n  1120\tversioned with the execution contract stamp (UCF §7.3.3) because the\n  1121\teffect a mnemonic raises is part of that contract. Extraction applies the\n  1122\ttable **before** any union with callable-profile effects, so the union is\n  1123\tover one vocabulary. The table for contract `\"v2\"`:\n  1124\t\n  1125\t+---------------+------------------------+--------------------+----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n  1126\t| Syntax tag    | Mnemonic               | Effect identifiers | Other requirement contributed                                                                            |\n  1127\t|               |                        | contributed        |                                                                                                          |\n  1128\t+===============+========================+====================+==========================================================================================================+\n  1129\t| `:stream/make | `:stream-make`         | `#{:stream/make}`  | —                                                                                                        |\n  1130\t| `             |                        |                    |                                                                                                          |\n  1131\t+---------------+------------------------+--------------------+----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n  1132\t| `:stream/put` | `:stream-put`          | `#{:stream/put}`   | —                                                                                                        |\n  1133\t+---------------+------------------------+--------------------+----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n  1134\t| `:stream/curs | `:stream-cursor`       | `#{:stream/cursor} | —                                                                                                        |\n  1135\t| or`           |                        | `                  |                                                                                                          |\n  1136\t+---------------+------------------------+--------------------+----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n  1137\t| `:stream/next | `:stream-next`         | `#{:stream/next}`  | —                                                                                                        |\n  1138\t| `             |                        |                    |                                                                                                          |\n  1139\t+---------------+------------------------+--------------------+----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n  1140\t| `:stream/clos | `:stream-close`        | `#{:stream/close}` | —                                                                                                        |\n  1141\t| e`            |                        |                    |                                                                                                          |\n  1142\t+---------------+------------------------+--------------------+----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n  1143\t| `:dao.stream. | `:ffi-call op`         | `#{}`: an FFI call | `op` into `:yin.k/ffi-ops`, which is a **receiver capability requirement**: the resumer must hold its    |\n  1144\t| apply/call    |                        | is not an effect   | own FFI pair (`ffi/require-call-pair!` is checked before every call, `semantic.cljc:405-415`) and a      |\n  1145\t| op`           |                        | kind               | bridge handler for `op`. The pair's store keys (`v2.cljc:135-147`) are **never** a store-slice           |\n  1146\t|               |                        |                    | requirement: UCF §7.6.2 excludes the pair from the slice and the resumer installs its own; outstanding   |\n  1147\t|               |                        |                    | calls route per UCF §7.4.3                                                                               |\n  1148\t+---------------+------------------------+--------------------+----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n  1149\t| `:vm/park`    | `:park`                | `#{}`:             | —                                                                                                        |\n  1150\t|               |                        | scheduler-internal |                                                                                                          |\n  1151\t+---------------+------------------------+--------------------+----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n  1152\t| `:vm/resume   | `:resume pid`          | `#{}`              | `pid` into the parked-record obligation                                                                  |\n  1153\t| pid`          |                        |                    |                                                                                                          |\n  1154\t+---------------+------------------------+--------------------+----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n  1155\t| `:vm/gensym`  | `:gensym`              | `#{}`: the id      | —                                                                                                        |\n  1156\t|               |                        | counter is machine |                                                                                                          |\n  1157\t|               |                        | state              |                                                                                                          |\n  1158\t+---------------+------------------------+--------------------+----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n  1159\t| `:vm/store-ge | `:store-get k`,        | `#{}`              | `k` into the store-slice requirement                                                                     |\n  1160\t| t k`,         | `:store-put k`         |                    |                                                                                                          |\n  1161\t| `:vm/store-pu |                        |                    |                                                                                                          |\n  1162\t| t k`          |                        |                    |                                                                                                          |\n  1163\t+---------------+------------------------+--------------------+----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n  1164\t| `:vm/current- | `:current-continuation | `#{}`              | —                                                                                                        |\n  1165\t| continuation` | `                      |                    |                                                                                                          |\n  1166\t+---------------+------------------------+--------------------+----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n  1167\t| `:global`     | `:global`              | `#{}`              | name into the name obligations                                                                           |\n  1168\t+---------------+------------------------+--------------------+----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n  1169\t| `:literal`,   | `:const`, `:var`,      | `#{}` from syntax; | —                                                                                                        |\n  1170\t| `:variable`,  | `:closure`, `:push`,   | a call's effects   |                                                                                                          |\n  1171\t| `:lambda`,    | `:call`, `:return`,    | are its callee's   |                                                                                                          |\n  1172\t| `:application | `:jump`,               | profile effects    |                                                                                                          |\n  1173\t| `, `:if`      | `:branch-false`,       | (§7.7.2)           |                                                                                                          |\n  1174\t|               | `:halt`                |                    |                                                                                                          |\n  1175\t+---------------+------------------------+--------------------+----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n  1176\t\n  1177\tEvery tag and every mnemonic has a row, so \"no external effect\" is an\n  1178\texplicit `#{}`, never an absence. The conformance obligation: for every\n  1179\tcorpus tree, the requirement set computed from the tree and the one\n  1180\tcomputed from its lowered segment are **equal** in every field of\n  1181\t`:yin.k/requires`. A tag or mnemonic outside the table is\n  1182\t`:yin.k/undecodable`, the same outcome the validators give it.\n  1183\t\n  1184\t#### 7.7.2 Name obligations are resolved per store slice, and completion is conservative\n  1185\t\n  1186\tA name extracted from code (a `:global` row) is an **obligation**. Because this design preserves named variables across the Semantic Tuple boundary, the evaluator simply looks up the name in the environment.\n  1187\t\n  1188\t**Variables never fall through to globals.** An under-arity call leaves missing parameter names bound to `nil`; an over-arity call drops extra arguments beyond the params list length. An unbound variable name evaluates to `nil`; it never falls through to the store, primitives, or modules (since `:variable` and `:global` are distinct nodes). Therefore, name obligations are strictly and statically the set of `:global` names, never `:variable` names.\n  1189\t\n  1190\tEvery retained obligation is a required primitive or module export, checked by\n  1191\tprofile (UCF §7.5.2), and the **effects of a callable are read from its\n  1192\tprofile** (`:yin.k/effects`), never inferred from the name: `yin/def`\n  1193\tcontributes `:vm/store-put` and `require` contributes `:module/require`\n  1194\tbecause their profiles say so. Where a retained name has no profile at the\n  1195\temitter, discovery is `:incomplete`; conservatively retaining every\n  1196\tpossible primitive and module requirement is always admissible and never\n  1197\tmakes a `:complete` result wrong.\n  1198\t\n  1199\tThis rule is the amendment UCF §7.6.1's *Names* paragraph needs: its\n  1200\tsentence that a name \"is satisfied if it is bound in any environment the\n  1201\tvalue carries\" is the unsound test, and this document states the\n  1202\tcorrect rule in its place; UCF must be amended to say the same\n  1203\t(§10.8).\n  1204\t\n  1205\t#### 7.7.3 The fixed point converges over work items, not addresses\n  1206\t\n  1207\tThe unit of analysis is a **work item** `[code-address context]`, not an\n  1208\taddress. Two closures sharing one segment address with different captured\n  1209\tenvironments are two work items, because each enters the segment with its\n  1210\town environment (`semantic.cljc:199-205`) and so reaches different values (closures, streams, cursors, parked records), which\n  1211\tcontribute different work items; the name obligations of the segment are the same for both. The **context** of a work item is a finite abstraction: the\n  1212\tclosure's captured positional environment, and nothing else. This context\n  1213\tcontributes values (such as closures or streams bound within it), not\n  1214\tname discharge. Because values are finite, the set of contexts is finite\n  1215\tand the iteration terminates.\n  1216\t\n  1217\t- **Code once, contexts each.** Code is fetched, validated, and projected\n  1218\t  once per address; every newly discovered `[address context]` pair is\n  1219\t  analyzed, whether or not the address was seen before.\n  1220\t- **Parked ids.** Each extracted parked id must name a record in the\n  1221\t  carried `:parked` slice (UCF §7.6.3); a missing one is `:yin.k/unsatisfied`\n  1222\t  naming the id.\n  1223\t- **Values.** Closures, streams, and cursors reachable from the frame, the\n  1224\t  K frames, parked records, the store slice, and the pending wait\n  1225\t  contribute work items and stream identities as §7.6.1 specifies; a\n  1226\t  closure contributes `[its segment address, its captured-env context]`.\n  1227\t- **Modules.** A required module with an undeclared footprint sets\n  1228\t  `:yin.k/discovery :incomplete`, as §7.6.1 already rules.\n  1229\t\n  1230\t**Convergence** is reached when one full pass adds nothing to any of:\n  1231\tthe work-item set, the obligation set, the discovered\n  1232\tvalue set (closures, streams, cursors, parked records), the store-key\n  1233\trequirement, the FFI-op requirement, the effect set after normalization\n  1234\t(§7.7.1), and the callable and module footprints. A pass that adds a new\n  1235\tcontext, a new profile fact, or a new footprint continues the iteration\n  1236\teven when no new address appeared.\n  1237\t\n  1238\tDiscovery is `:complete` only when every reachable work item was\n  1239\tanalyzed, every `:global` obligation was discharged by the store slice by key or retained\n  1240\tas a profiled primitive or module requirement, every parked id resolved, and every module\n  1241\tfootprint was declared. It is `:blocked` when an address cannot be\n  1242\tfetched and `:incomplete` otherwise. Recasting the walk as Datalog changes\n  1243\twhere the facts come from, not the conservatism of the closure.\n  1244\t\n  1245\tUCF §7.6.1 has the same defect in its own terms: \"repeats until nothing\n  1246\tnew is reachable\" is stated over segments and values, and its walk of \"a\n  1247\tnewly fetched segment\" is per address. It needs this same amendment\n  1248\t(§10.8).\n  1249\t\n  1250\tBecause the queries are over data, they answer for a tree that has never\n  1251\trun, which is the property §7.6.1 names.\n  1252\t\n  1253\t---\n  1254\t\n  1255\t## §8 The ledger and refs\n  1256\t\n  1257\t### 8.1 Two notations, one relation\n  1258\t\n  1259\tThe ledger is one logical relation with rows `[t op address ref]`, ruling\n  1260\t6's shape: `t` is the ledger's clock, `op` is drawn from an open\n  1261\tvocabulary, `address` is a content address, and `ref` is the row's other\n  1262\targument, a name entity or a second address. This document writes ledger\n  1263\tfacts in that notation when it speaks of the relation.\n  1264\t\n  1265\tThe **persistent representation** of a row is datoms, and a datom is bound\n  1266\tby `datom.md`'s contract: `e`, `t`, and `m` are stream-local, `e` is a\n  1267\tnon-negative integer, `m` is an integer, and `v` is the only slot that may\n  1268\thold a foreign reference (`datom.md:140-180`). The transactor enforces\n  1269\tthis: `local-datom?` (`src/cljc/dao/datom.cljc:42-58`) rejects a\n  1270\tnon-integer `e` or `m`, and `pad-datom`\n  1271\t(`src/cljc/dao/space/transactor.cljc:74-90`) rejects a caller-supplied `t`\n  1272\tand stamps its own. Therefore a content address never appears in `e`, an\n  1273\top keyword never appears in `m`, and `t` is never chosen by the writer.\n  1274\tThe mapping between notations is:\n  1275\t\n  1276\t+--------------+---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n  1277\t| Logical slot | Persistent carrier                                                                                                                                      |\n  1278\t+==============+=========================================================================================================================================================+\n  1279\t| `t`          | the datom `t` the transactor stamped                                                                                                                    |\n  1280\t+--------------+---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n  1281\t| `op`         | for naming rows: the reserved validity marker in `m` (`:db/assert` 1, `:db/retract` 0); for every other op: the `:yin.ledger/op` value of a **ledger    |\n  1282\t|              | event entity** (§8.2)                                                                                                                                   |\n  1283\t+--------------+---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n  1284\t| `address`    | a `v` slot                                                                                                                                              |\n  1285\t+--------------+---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n  1286\t| `ref`        | for naming rows: the local name entity in `e`; for address-to-address rows: a second `v` slot of the same event entity                                  |\n  1287\t+--------------+---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n  1288\t\n  1289\tAnything written in the `[t op address ref]` notation below is a logical\n  1290\trow; anything written as `[e a v t m]` is transactor input, with `e` a\n  1291\tlocal integer or tempid, `t` nil at write time, and `m` a reserved marker.\n  1292\t\n  1293\t### 8.2 Ledger records and ledger events\n  1294\t\n  1295\tEvery non-naming ledger fact is first a **ledger record**: a plain map,\n  1296\tcontent-addressed by `segment-key`, stored in `dao.jing` like any other content.\n  1297\tRecord shapes:\n  1298\t\n  1299\t+------------+-------------------------------------------------------+---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n  1300\t| Op         | Record                                                | Meaning                                                                                           |\n  1301\t+============+=======================================================+===================================================================================================+\n  1302\t| `:derive`  | `{:yin.ledger/op :derive :yin.ledger/input tree-addr  | `output` was computed from `input` by `function` under the lowering profile (§5.2.1)              |\n  1303\t|            | :yin.ledger/output seg-addr :yin.ledger/function      |                                                                                                   |\n  1304\t|            | fn-name :yin.ledger/profile profile-map}`             |                                                                                                   |\n  1305\t+------------+-------------------------------------------------------+---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n  1306\t| `:expand`  | `{:yin.ledger/op :expand :yin.ledger/input tree-in    | one expansion attempt; exactly one of `:yin.ledger/origin` (initial) and `:yin.ledger/parent`     |\n  1307\t|            | :yin.ledger/call-root tree-in :yin.ledger/call-path   | (nested) is present; failures carry `:yin.ledger/error` instead of `:yin.ledger/output` (§8.4).   |\n  1308\t|            | path :yin.ledger/origin [:source medium batch j]`     | `:yin.ledger/attempt` is the portable attempt identity of §8.4.1, scoped to the expander          |\n  1309\t|            | **or** `:yin.ledger/parent parent-record-addr`        | incarnation, so two expanders or two executions of one expander observing one call never mint one |\n  1310\t|            | `:yin.ledger/output tree-out :yin.ledger/macro        | record. The record holds no log-local id: a nested attempt names its parent by the parent's       |\n  1311\t|            | lambda-addr :yin.ledger/attempt [incarnation          | record **address**, so the record is minted only after the parent's record exists                 |\n  1312\t|            | counter]}`                                            |                                                                                                   |\n  1313\t+------------+-------------------------------------------------------+---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n  1314\t| `:publish` | `{:yin.ledger/op :publish :yin.ledger/subject         | a checkpoint occurrence was published as the UCF value at `output` (UCF §7.3.4)                   |\n  1315\t|            | occurrence-id :yin.ledger/output ucf-addr}`           |                                                                                                   |\n  1316\t+------------+-------------------------------------------------------+---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n  1317\t\n  1318\tA record is a content-addressed value, so it is immutable, addressable, and verifiable,\n  1319\tand it is what \"the hash chain\" is made of: a record names its input and\n  1320\toutput by address, and the record's own address is what the ledger\n  1321\tpublishes. Walking the chain is following record addresses; code content\n  1322\tnever has to know its predecessor.\n  1323\t\n  1324\tA record enters the ledger as a **ledger event entity**, a local entity\n  1325\t(id ≥ `dao.datom/first-user-id`, `datom.cljc:27-40`) with the record's\n  1326\tfields as attributes and the record's address as one more:\n  1327\t\n  1328\t```clojure\n  1329\t[[:db/add ev :yin.ledger/op       :derive]\n  1330\t [:db/add ev :yin.ledger/input    tree-addr]\n  1331\t [:db/add ev :yin.ledger/output   seg-addr]\n  1332\t [:db/add ev :yin.ledger/function :yin.vm/lower]\n  1333\t [:db/add ev :yin.ledger/profile  {:yin.lower/profile \"ast-v1\" :yin.code/contract \"v2\" :yin.k/version 0}]\n  1334\t [:db/add ev :yin.ledger/record   record-addr]]     ; ev is a tempid; m defaults to :db/assert\n  1335\t```\n  1336\t\n  1337\tThe profile is carried as the structured map, exactly as it appears in the\n  1338\trecord. `local-datom?` constrains `e`, `a`, `t`, and `m` and places no\n  1339\trestriction on `v` (`datom.cljc:42-58`), and today's schema already\n  1340\tcarries a vector in `v`; no string\n  1341\trendering of the profile is defined.\n  1342\t\n  1343\tEvent operation and fact validity are separate by construction. The op is\n  1344\ta namespaced keyword in a `v` slot, globally meaningful without a\n  1345\treserved id, which is `datom.md`'s own rule for built-ins that live in `a`\n  1346\tand `v` (`datom.md:214-217`); the datoms of the event carry the ordinary\n  1347\t`:db/assert` marker and fold as ordinary facts. No reserved `m` id is\n  1348\tallocated, because reserved ids have one global meaning everywhere\n  1349\t(`datom.md:200-212`) and a composition cannot mint one. Where a\n  1350\tcomposition wants an event to be the provenance of a naming fact, the\n  1351\tnaming datom's `m` names the event entity through the established\n  1352\tmetadata-entity mechanism, an id ≥ 16 carrying `:db/op` and provenance\n  1353\t(`datom.cljc:2-7`, `datom.md:172-180`; the transactor's map-`m` input at\n  1354\t`src/cljc/dao/space/transact.cljc:15-29`). A fact whose `m` names a\n  1355\tmetadata entity is live under the current fold, which removes only\n  1356\t`:db/retract` markers (`query.cljc:76-103`); a validity op other than\n  1357\tassert or retract is expressed by the reserved marker, never by the\n  1358\tevent, so the fold needs no knowledge of the open vocabulary. Interpreting\n  1359\ta metadata entity's `:db/op` for Datomic-visible validity is the\n  1360\tprerequisite `dao.space.schema.datomic.md` §4 names and is outside this\n  1361\tdesign.\n  1362\t\n  1363\tThe vocabulary is open: a composition that needs a new op declares a new\n  1364\tkeyword and a record shape. A reader dispatches on the ops it understands\n  1365\t(`[?ev :yin.ledger/op :derive]`) and does not see the others, which is a\n  1366\tproperty of the query it writes, not of the fold.\n  1367\t\n  1368\t### 8.3 Rows of the one relation\n  1369\t\n  1370\t+-----------------------------------------------+------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n  1371\t| Logical row                                   | Persistent form                                                                                                        |\n  1372\t+===============================================+========================================================================================================================+\n  1373\t| `[t :assert tree-addr name]` — a name refers  | `[:db/add name :yin/code tree-addr]`                                                                                   |\n  1374\t| to a tree                                     |                                                                                                                        |\n  1375\t+-----------------------------------------------+------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n  1376\t| `[t :retract tree-addr name]` — the name no   | `[:db/retract name :yin/code tree-addr]`, followed by the new assertion                                                |\n  1377\t| longer does                                   |                                                                                                                        |\n  1378\t+-----------------------------------------------+------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n  1379\t| `[t :derive seg-addr tree-addr]` — a segment  | a `:derive` event entity (§8.2); direction: `input` is the tree, `output` is the segment                               |\n  1380\t| was lowered from a tree                       |                                                                                                                        |\n  1381\t+-----------------------------------------------+------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n  1382\t| `[t :assert seg-addr k-occurrence]` — a       | `[:db/add occ :yin.k/segment seg-addr]` on the local occurrence entity                                                 |\n  1383\t| segment is the address a continuation names   |                                                                                                                        |\n  1384\t+-----------------------------------------------+------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n  1385\t| `[t :expand tree-out tree-in]` — a tree is    | an `:expand` event entity (§8.4)                                                                                       |\n  1386\t| the expansion of another                      |                                                                                                                        |\n  1387\t+-----------------------------------------------+------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n  1388\t| `[t :assert lambda-addr name]` under          | not a ledger row at all: macro-ness is an occurrence-bound declaration beside the tree (§8.5); the ledger records only |\n  1389\t| `:yin.macro/definition`                       | the expander's harvest result, `[:db/add name :yin.macro/definition lambda-addr]`, for queries                         |\n  1390\t+-----------------------------------------------+------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n  1391\t| `[t :publish ucf-addr occurrence]` — a        | a `:publish` event entity; custody stays `dao.lease.md`'s vocabulary unchanged (UCF §7.7.2), keyed by the same local   |\n  1392\t| checkpoint occurrence was published           | occurrence entity                                                                                                      |\n  1393\t+-----------------------------------------------+------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n  1394\t| `[t :assert tree-addr manifest]` — a module   | `[:db/add manifest :yin.module/exports tree-addr]`                                                                     |\n  1395\t| manifest pins a tree                          |                                                                                                                        |\n  1396\t+-----------------------------------------------+------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n  1397\t\n  1398\t`:yin.code/derived-from` therefore keeps its name as a query alias for\n  1399\t\"the `:yin.ledger/input` of a `:derive` event whose output is this\n  1400\taddress\" and is no longer an attribute of the segment (`v2.cljc:327`,\n  1401\t`linearize.cljc:212`).\n  1402\t\n  1403\t### 8.4 Expansion events keep their identity\n  1404\t\n  1405\t`yin.vm.macro.md` §4.1 records one event per attempt, successful or not,\n  1406\tincluding repeated identical expansions, and links the event to its output\n  1407\t(`yin.vm.macro.md:666-709`). This design keeps that contract: the\n  1408\t`:expand` event entity **is** that event, one per attempt, and the address\n  1409\trows are attributes of it, not a replacement for it:\n  1410\t\n  1411\t```clojure\n  1412\t[[:db/add ev :yin.ledger/op         :expand]\n  1413\t [:db/add ev :yin.ledger/input      tree-in-addr]        ; the tree containing the call\n  1414\t [:db/add ev :yin.ledger/call-root  tree-in-addr]        ; the call occurrence, as values:\n  1415\t [:db/add ev :yin.ledger/call-path  path]                ;   root address and structural path\n  1416\t [:db/add ev :yin.ledger/origin     [:source medium batch j]]   ; INITIAL expansion only: a value\n  1417\t [:db/add ev :yin.ledger/parent-event parent-ev]         ; NESTED expansion only: a declared ref, log-local\n  1418\t [:db/add ev :yin.ledger/parent     parent-record-addr]  ; NESTED expansion only: the parent's record address, portable\n  1419\t [:db/add ev :yin.ledger/output     tree-out-addr]       ; on success\n  1420\t [:db/add ev :yin.ledger/macro      lambda-addr]         ; the macro's content\n  1421\t [:db/add ev :yin.ledger/attempt    [incarnation n]]     ; portable attempt identity, §8.4.1\n  1422\t [:db/add ev :yin.ledger/record     record-addr]         ; the addressed record of §8.2\n  1423\t [:db/add ev :yin/source-batch      batch-id]            ; unchanged from §4.1\n  1424\t [:db/add ev :yin/error             {...}]               ; on failure, unchanged\n  1425\t [:db/add ev :yin/timestamp         ctx-t]]              ; unchanged\n  1426\t```\n  1427\t\n  1428\tThe split between local references and portable values follows\n  1429\t`yin.vm.macro.md` §4.1 exactly (`yin.vm.macro.md:692-708`), and it is\n  1430\tenforced by how the transactor resolves ids: `apply-tempid-map` replaces a\n  1431\ttempid in `v` **only when the whole value is a tempid on a declared\n  1432\treference attribute** (`src/cljc/dao/space/transact.cljc:168-185`); a\n  1433\ttempid nested inside a vector is left as it is. Therefore:\n  1434\t\n  1435\t- an **initial** expansion's call sits in admitted syntax, so it carries\n  1436\t  `:yin.ledger/origin`, a value `[:source medium batch j]` including the\n  1437\t  batch member index: the successor of `:yin/source-call` qualified by\n  1438\t  `:yin/source-batch`, which no transactor resolves, as today;\n  1439\t- a **nested** expansion's call sits in the output of the enclosing\n  1440\t  expansion, so it carries **two** links to the parent: `:yin.ledger/\n  1441\t  parent-event`, a declared reference attribute whose whole value is the\n  1442\t  parent event's id, the successor of `:yin/source-node` and resolved by\n  1443\t  the transactor within the log medium exactly as `:yin/source-node` is\n  1444\t  today; and `:yin.ledger/parent`, the parent's **record address**, which\n  1445\t  is a value and travels. The parent is complete before the child begins,\n  1446\t  because a nested expansion's input is the enclosing expansion's output\n  1447\t  (`yin.vm.macro.md:698-701`), so the parent's record address is known\n  1448\t  when the child's record is minted; no staging after allocation is\n  1449\t  needed, and the record (§8.2) never contains a log-local id, so it is\n  1450\t  hashed once, when built.\n  1451\t\n  1452\tA successful event carries exactly one of `:yin.ledger/origin` and the\n  1453\tparent pair; an admission failure carries neither. The chain `source →\n  1454\tevent → output → event → output` of §4.1 is walked locally through\n  1455\t`:yin.ledger/parent-event` and portably through `:yin.ledger/parent`, and\n  1456\ttwo attempts that produced identical output trees are two events with two\n  1457\tids and two record addresses, because their `:yin.ledger/attempt` values\n  1458\tdiffer by construction (§8.4.1), so a child names its parent without\n  1459\tambiguity in either walk. `:yin/expansion-root` and `:yin/macro` of §4.1 are carried by\n  1460\t`:yin.ledger/output` and `:yin.ledger/macro` as addresses; the other event\n  1461\tattributes are unchanged. Because the *content* values are addresses, the\n  1462\tcross-media identity problem of `yin.vm.macro.md` §4.2 does not arise for\n  1463\tcontent: the same tree has the same address on `program-in`,\n  1464\t`program-out`, and the log. The local event id remains log-local, as\n  1465\t`:yin/source-node` is today; the record address is what leaves the log.\n  1466\t\n  1467\t#### 8.4.1 Attempt identity is scoped to an expander incarnation\n  1468\t\n  1469\tA record's address is the hash of its value, so two attempts whose records\n  1470\tare value-equal are one record, whatever the encoder. A bare per-expander\n  1471\tcounter does not prevent that: two expanders observing one medium, batch,\n  1472\tmember, and call path, each at counter `0`, with one macro and one output,\n  1473\twould write one record; so would one expander restarted and replaying the\n  1474\tbatch. The governing contract records every attempt separately\n  1475\t(`yin.vm.macro.md:668-669`), so the attempt identity must be unique per\n  1476\tattempt across expanders and across executions. It is:\n  1477\t\n  1478\t```clojure\n  1479\t:yin.ledger/attempt [incarnation counter]\n  1480\t```\n  1481\t\n  1482\t- **The incarnation** is a value held in the expander's process state\n  1483\t  `ctx` (`yin.vm.macro.md:216-223`), as a new field `:incarnation`,\n  1484\t  threaded through every call exactly as `:alloc` and `:t` are. It is\n  1485\t  **allocated**, never observed: a cursor read reserves nothing, since a\n  1486\t  cursor is a position minted by the stream for observation\n  1487\t  (`dao.stream.md:471-477`) and the memory log's `:dao.stream/newest`\n  1488\t  cursor is simply its current value count\n  1489\t  (`src/cljc/dao/stream/memory_log.cljc:105-110`), so two constructions\n  1490\t  with no append between them read one position. Nor does committing a\n  1491\t  record on the log allocate anything: `prepare-tx` is a pure function of\n  1492\t  the history it is handed (`src/cljc/dao/space/transact.cljc:189-207`),\n  1493\t  so two preparations against one history assign one permanent id, and\n  1494\t  `transact!` allocates only `t` under its lock and appends datoms that\n  1495\t  are already resolved (`src/cljc/dao/space/transactor.cljc:231-254`);\n  1496\t  two transactor values over one stream are a documented single-writer\n  1497\t  hazard, not a coordination (`dao.space.transactor.md`, T6). **The log\n  1498\t  therefore plays no part in incarnation identity.** The incarnation is a\n  1499\t  **composition-minted token**, the one mechanism for every construction:\n  1500\t\n  1501\t  ```clojure\n  1502\t  :incarnation {:yin.expander/token <token>}\n  1503\t  ```\n  1504\t\n  1505\t  where `<token>` is a fresh random UUID minted by the host's library at\n  1506\t  the moment of construction (`random-uuid`, as the ring-buffer transport\n  1507\t  already mints its stream identity, `src/cljc/dao/stream/ringbuffer.cljc:27`).\n  1508\t  The uniqueness warranty is the composition's, discharged by the token's\n  1509\t  122 random bits: it covers two expanders on one log, two expanders on\n  1510\t  two logs, and one expander constructed twice, with or without a\n  1511\t  restart, and it needs no state that survives a restart, which is why a\n  1512\t  random token and not a counter is the rule; a durable counter would\n  1513\t  need an allocate-and-persist boundary of its own, which is the problem\n  1514\t  being avoided. No id allocated by any transactor, and no position of\n  1515\t  any stream, enters the incarnation.\n  1516\t- **Allocation retry.** Construction is all-or-nothing, as it is for a VM\n  1517\t  (`v2.cljc:21-23`): minting the token is the first act of construction,\n  1518\t  and a construction that fails afterward, for any reason, leaves no\n  1519\t  expander. A construction that is retried mints a **new token** and\n  1520\t  obtains a new incarnation; it never reuses or reconstructs an earlier\n  1521\t  one, because an incarnation is reachable only through the `ctx` that\n  1522\t  holds it, and a `ctx` that was not retained is gone. A token under\n  1523\t  which no attempt was ever recorded is harmless.\n  1524\t- **The counter** is a per-incarnation attempt counter in `ctx`, advanced\n  1525\t  once per attempt, initial or nested, successful or failed, exactly as\n  1526\t  `:alloc` advances (`yin.vm.macro.md:360-365`); admission-failure events\n  1527\t  advance it too, since they are attempts under §4.1.\n  1528\t- **A staged retry reuses the identity; a distinct execution receives\n  1529\t  another.** The expander stages `expand-batch`'s output and log payloads\n  1530\t  and retries a failed flush from the staged data without re-running the\n  1531\t  expansion (`yin.vm.macro.md:364-365`, `:765-773`): the record, and the\n  1532\t  attempt identity inside it, are part of what was staged, so the retry\n  1533\t  writes the same record to the same address, which is the idempotent\n  1534\t  re-publication the ledger wants. A restart constructs a new expander,\n  1535\t  which mints a new incarnation, so replaying a batch after a restart\n  1536\t  mints new attempt identities and new records: that is a distinct\n  1537\t  execution and is recorded as one. What separates the two cases is\n  1538\t  whether `ctx` survived: a retry runs under the `ctx` that produced the\n  1539\t  staged payload; a restart does not have it, and allocates (above).\n  1540\t\n  1541\tThe parent-record chain of §8.4 is unchanged; it gains uniqueness from the\n  1542\tattempt identity inside every record.\n  1543\t\n  1544\t### 8.5 Macro declarations travel beside the tree\n  1545\t\n  1546\tThe `:macro?` flag is outside the canonical tree (§2.5). What replaces it\n  1547\tis a **declaration fact bound to an occurrence**, produced by the frontend\n  1548\tand consumed by the expander at admission. The governing contract admits\n  1549\tand harvests **every entity in the batch, in batch order, including\n  1550\tdefinitions disconnected from the execution root**\n  1551\t(`yin.vm.macro.md:229-249`). A single rooted tree cannot carry a\n  1552\tdisconnected definition, so the composition between frontend and expander\n  1553\tis an **ordered batch**, not a tree:\n  1554\t\n  1555\t```clojure\n  1556\t{:yin/batch         [<rows-0> <rows-1> … <rows-n>]   ; canonical row sets, one per admitted root or disconnected subgraph\n  1557\t :yin/root          i                                ; index of the tree the evaluator runs\n  1558\t :yin/declarations  #{[j path :yin.macro/definition] …}   ; the definition at path in tree j is a macro\n  1559\t :yin/harvest       [#{[j path] …} #{[j' path'] …} …]}   ; one entry per ORIGINAL definition, in admission order;\n  1560\t                                                     ;   each entry is the set of [j path] occurrences of that definition\n  1561\t```\n  1562\t\n  1563\tRules:\n  1564\t\n  1565\t- **Harvest order is an explicit catalogue, not a traversal.** The\n  1566\t  governing contract harvests every definition in the batch **in the\n  1567\t  order its entities appeared in the admitted datom batch**\n  1568\t  (`yin.vm.macro.md:242-249`), and it admits any acyclic, internally\n  1569\t  resolved batch; nothing requires entity order to equal structural\n  1570\t  preorder, and a root may contain definitions A then B whose entities\n  1571\t  arrived as B then A. Vector order plus preorder therefore does **not**\n  1572\t  preserve that contract, and this document does not claim it does. The\n  1573\t  catalogue `:yin/harvest` is the preserved order: a vector with **one\n  1574\t  entry per original definition**, in the original admission order, each\n  1575\t  entry the set of `[j path]` occurrences at which that definition\n  1576\t  appears in the batch's trees, connected or not. Initial harvest\n  1577\t  (`yin.vm.macro.md` §3.1 step 2) iterates the catalogue, last wins; it\n  1578\t  never iterates the trees. For a batch converted from datoms by the §9.1\n  1579\t  adapter the catalogue is derived from entity order, so the legacy\n  1580\t  contract is preserved exactly. For a batch a tuple-native\n  1581\t  frontend emits, the frontend chooses the order it writes; the reference\n  1582\t  frontends write source order, which is the **declaration order** of\n  1583\t  `yin.vm.macro.md` §3.2 (`yin.vm.macro.md:298-304`: operands before the\n  1584\t  body of an immediately applied lambda). That is a new contract for a\n  1585\t  new producer, not a narrowing of the legacy one, and the two are kept\n  1586\t  distinct: post-expansion declaration order is what the expander uses\n  1587\t  for definitions its own output forms (§3.1 step 5), and it stays\n  1588\t  separate from the admission catalogue.\n  1589\t- **One original definition, many occurrences, one ordinal.** A legacy\n  1590\t  batch may hold one definition entity reached through several\n  1591\t  references: the codec emits a pre-assigned entity once and lets every\n  1592\t  referring site name it (`v2.cljc:394-411`, `seen-eids`), and\n  1593\t  `yang.clojure` produces exactly this for a defmacro lambda used at its\n  1594\t  definition and at call sites (`clojure.cljc:418-437`). Inlining that\n  1595\t  graph into the batch's trees produces several `[j path]` occurrences of\n  1596\t  one definition. The old harvest and declaration catalogue number the\n  1597\t  **entity**, giving it one declaration ordinal `k`, and a transformer\n  1598\t  may fabricate a stand-in that selects a catalogue entry by `k`\n  1599\t  (`yin.vm.macro.md:254-275`), so the ordinal is observable and\n  1600\t  duplicating it would be wrong. Therefore a catalogue entry is an\n  1601\t  **occurrence group**: the definition is harvested once, and the\n  1602\t  catalogue, not the trees, is the identity of the definition. The\n  1603\t  adapter derives groups from entity identity; a tuple-native frontend\n  1604\t  emits singleton groups, one per definition it writes, and if it writes\n  1605\t  one definition at several places it emits one group naming them all.\n  1606\t- **Two sequences: harvest order and declaration ordinals.** The\n  1607\t  governing contract numbers **only source macro definitions** into\n  1608\t  `:declared`, in harvest order, and replaces only those by stand-ins;\n  1609\t  plain definitions stay executable `yin/def` nodes and are forwarded\n  1610\t  untouched (`yin.vm.macro.md:254-278`). The harvest catalogue above\n  1611\t  contains every definition group, plain and macro alike, because harvest\n  1612\t  and redefinition need all of them in admission order. The **declaration\n  1613\t  catalogue** is a second, derived sequence: the macro-declared groups\n  1614\t  only (those whose occurrences carry `:yin.macro/definition`), in the\n  1615\t  relative order they have in the harvest catalogue. A macro group's\n  1616\t  declaration ordinal `k` is its index in the declaration catalogue, never\n  1617\t  its index in the harvest catalogue; every occurrence of that group is\n  1618\t  replaced by a stand-in carrying that same `:decl k` (§3.1 step 3), so a\n  1619\t  fabricated stand-in selects the same entry it would select under the\n  1620\t  legacy expander. A plain definition preceding macro `m` therefore leaves\n  1621\t  `m` at ordinal `0`, as today. Plain groups participate in harvest and\n  1622\t  redefinition (§3.1 step 2's last-wins over the harvest catalogue,\n  1623\t  including a plain redefinition removing a macro) and are **never**\n  1624\t  replaced by stand-ins; their occurrences remain ordinary `yin/def`\n  1625\t  syntax in the trees the evaluator receives.\n  1626\t- **The catalogue is validated at admission.** Every occurrence in every\n  1627\t  group must name a `(yin/def <literal sym> _)` node; every such node in\n  1628\t  the batch must appear in exactly one group; groups are non-empty and\n  1629\t  pairwise disjoint; every occurrence in a group must have the same\n  1630\t  `:literal` symbol and the same value-operand content address, since one\n  1631\t  original definition cannot have two bodies; every `:yin/declarations`\n  1632\t  entry must be a member of some group, and a group is declared as a whole\n  1633\t  (all of its occurrences carry the declaration or none do, which is\n  1634\t  automatic for a legacy entity with one flag). The declaration catalogue\n  1635\t  is derived from the validated harvest catalogue and the declarations,\n  1636\t  never carried separately, so it cannot disagree with either. A\n  1637\t  violation is an admission failure `{:kind :malformed-input :reason\n  1638\t  :harvest-catalogue}` naming the entry.\n  1639\t- **Binding.** A declaration `[j path :yin.macro/definition]` names the\n  1640\t  `:application` at `path` in tree `j`, and through the catalogue the\n  1641\t  original definition whose group contains `[j path]`; that node must be\n  1642\t  `(yin/def <literal sym> <lambda>)`, i.e., its reconstructed semantic map must have an `:operator` that is a `:global` node naming `yin/def`, its first operand a `:literal` symbol node, and its second operand a `:lambda` node. A\n  1643\t  declaration whose coordinates do not resolve to such a node is an\n  1644\t  admission failure, `{:kind :malformed-input :reason\n  1645\t  :stray-macro-declaration}`, the successor of §3.1 step 1's\n  1646\t  `:stray-macro-lambda`.\n  1647\t- **Marks are validated before they are stripped.** A frontend or the\n  1648\t  §9.1 adapter that converts a map AST carrying `:macro? true` must reject\n  1649\t  a flagged lambda anywhere but the value operand of a `yin/def` as\n  1650\t  `:stray-macro-lambda` **at conversion**, before the flag is removed;\n  1651\t  it may never strip such a flag and emit a plain lambda. A flag in the\n  1652\t  admitted position becomes the declaration at that tree and path. The\n  1653\t  quiet case `yin.vm.macro.md` decision 5 exists to prevent is therefore\n  1654\t  prevented at the same point it is today.\n  1655\t- **Ordering of harvest.** The catalogue's order; last wins, as §3.1\n  1656\t  step 2 rules. Vector order and preorder play no part in harvest.\n  1657\t- **Missing fact.** A `(yin/def sym v)` with no declaration at its\n  1658\t  coordinates is a plain definition. If `sym` is currently a macro, the\n  1659\t  plain definition removes it, exactly as §3.1 step 2 rules.\n  1660\t- **Scope.** Harvest is syntactic and whole-batch, as today; a declaration\n  1661\t  in an unexecuted branch or in a tree the root never reaches is in force\n  1662\t  for the batch. Declarations are per batch and never persist beyond the\n  1663\t  harvest that consumed them; the harvested store is the expander's state.\n  1664\t- **Redefinition.** Two batches that carry identical trees differ in\n  1665\t  behaviour only through their declarations, which is the intended\n  1666\t  separation: content is what the program is; being a macro is what this\n  1667\t  occurrence of a definition is *for*.\n  1668\t- **Evaluators see the root only.** The composition hands an evaluator\n  1669\t  `(nth batch root)` after expansion; a tree outside the root is expander\n  1670\t  input, never program.\n  1671\t\n  1672\tThis replacement protocol, not the expander itself, is the blocker\n  1673\t(§10.5): tuple evaluation without macros needs none of it, and the v2\n  1674\texpander does not exist yet.\n  1675\t\n  1676\t### 8.6 As-of queries are ledger views\n  1677\t\n  1678\tCurrent value of a name uses the implemented current-state interpreter,\n  1679\twhich resolves the greatest `t` per `[e a v]` and then removes retractions\n  1680\t(`query/current`, `query.cljc:204-212`; the fold `current-state-seq`,\n  1681\t`query.cljc:76-103`):\n  1682\t\n  1683\t```clojure\n  1684\t(q '[:find ?addr . :in $ ?name :where [?e :yin/name ?name] [?e :yin/code ?addr]]\n  1685\t   (query/current refs) 'my.ns/f)\n  1686\t```\n  1687\t\n  1688\tValue of a name as of `?t` is the same view with its as-of argument, which\n  1689\tbounds visible datoms to `t ≤ ?t` before folding\n  1690\t(`query/current` two-arity, `query.cljc:210-212`; `bound-datoms`,\n  1691\t`query.cljc:68-73`):\n  1692\t\n  1693\t```clojure\n  1694\t(q '[:find ?addr . :in $ ?name :where [?e :yin/name ?name] [?e :yin/code ?addr]]\n  1695\t   (query/current refs t) 'my.ns/f)\n  1696\t```\n  1697\t\n  1698\tA hand-written negation over raw d5 rows is not the contract and is not\n  1699\tused: \"any retraction before `?t`\" wrongly removes an address that was\n  1700\tretracted and later reasserted, whereas the fold's latest-event-wins per\n  1701\t`[e a v]` keeps it. A query that must inspect raw rows selects the latest\n  1702\tevent per `[e a v]` with `max ?t` first and interprets that row's `m`\n  1703\tsecond.\n  1704\t\n  1705\tProvenance walks are joins over event entities:\n  1706\t\n  1707\t```clojure\n  1708\t;; what was this segment lowered from, and what did that expand from\n  1709\t[:find ?src\n  1710\t :in $ ?seg\n  1711\t :where [?d :yin.ledger/op :derive] [?d :yin.ledger/output ?seg] [?d :yin.ledger/input ?tree]\n  1712\t        [?x :yin.ledger/op :expand] [?x :yin.ledger/output ?tree] [?x :yin.ledger/input ?src]]\n  1713\t```\n  1714\t\n  1715\t---\n  1716\t\n  1717\t## §9 Migration surface\n  1718\t\n  1719\t### 9.1 The boundary, not a sweep\n  1720\t\n  1721\tThe walker stays on map ASTs exactly as built, retaining named variable evaluation. What changes is the\n  1722\tboundary around them — the projection in, the loader and the readers of\n  1723\trows behind it. The semantics do not change; the boundary does.\n  1724\t\n  1725\t+--------------------------------------------------+---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n  1726\t| Site                                             | Change                                                                                                              |\n  1727\t+==================================================+=====================================================================================================================+\n  1728\t| walker, `ast_walker.cljc`                        | Retains named variable evaluation (`:name`). `:lambda` arm binds by name. Does not use De Bruijn numbering. Adds `:stream/close` arm (§3.2).                                                                                                      |\n  1729\t+--------------------------------------------------+---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n  1730\t| loader, new                                      | validate rows (§7.4), reconstruct the map AST: `rows → map` (§6.1), the successor of `datoms->ast`                  |\n  1731\t|                                                  | (`v2.cljc:494-559`) with content addresses in place of allocated ids                                                |\n  1732\t+--------------------------------------------------+---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n  1733\t| linearizer,                                      | reads the row relation through the same `get-attr`-over-an-index shape it has today (`lower-node`'s `(get-attr e    |\n  1734\t| `linearize.cljc:87-148`,                         | :yin/type)` becomes a lookup by row id); `ast-children` becomes a table lookup of `node`/`nodes` slot positions;    |\n  1735\t| `:230-242`                                       | `lower` takes a row set and returns a vector plus the §5.3 provenance table; emits `:var name`, `:global name`, and `:closure params body`; `lower-ast` (`:245-252`) goes away     |\n  1736\t+--------------------------------------------------+---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n  1737\t| codec, `v2.cljc:372-559`                         | becomes `yin.vm/ast->semantic-bytecode`, the projection pair of §6.5 mapping Universal AST to Canonical Rows/Datoms.            |\n  1738\t|                                                  | `:yin/address` added; `:global` node added; retains `:yin/params` on lambda and `:yin/name` on variable |\n  1739\t+--------------------------------------------------+---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n  1740\t| `code/mnemonics`, `code/well-formed?`,           | `code/mnemonics` (`code.cljc:12-16`) adds `:global`. `well-formed?` keeps judging datom batches on the projection path before projection; the shared vector validator of §7.5 runs on   |\n  1741\t| `code.cljc:12-180`                               | both paths after it                                                                                                 |\n  1742\t+--------------------------------------------------+---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n  1743\t| `semantic/load-image`,                           | decodes from the vector directly on the primary path; positional frame binding for `:closure`; `:global` opcode resolves store → primitives → modules; decodes the new operands (`:var name`, `:global name`, `:closure params body`); both paths call the §7.5 validator   |\n  1744\t| `semantic.cljc:524-596`                          |                                                                                                                     |\n  1745\t+--------------------------------------------------+---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n  1746\t| frontends                                        | keep emitting map ASTs to the stream (as named universal ASTs). They do not perform the tuple projection. |\n  1747\t| `src/cljc/yang/clojure.cljc`,                    | All projection to Semantic Tuples and side-tables is deferred to the Encoder Observer (`yin.vm/ast->semantic-bytecode`).        |\n  1748\t| `python.cljc`, `php.cljc`                        |                                                                                                                     |\n  1749\t|                                                  | never survive the boundary. The r7 adapter's ordering rules become the standing contract: first reject any `:macro? |\n  1750\t|                                                  | true` lambda that is not the value operand of a `yin/def` as `:stray-macro-lambda`, then turn each admitted one into|\n  1751\t|                                                  | a declaration at its tree index and path, then strip the flag                                                       |\n  1752\t+--------------------------------------------------+---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n  1753\t| datom-batch adapter,                             | for media that carry datoms: derives `:yin/harvest` from entity order and entity identity while inlining, recording |\n  1754\t| standing                                         | every `[j path]` at which each definition entity is emitted (the `datoms->ast` recursion, `v2.cljc:494-559`,        |\n  1755\t|                                                  | extended to carry the path and the entity id, since today it tracks neither); one entity with several paths becomes |\n  1756\t|                                                  | one occurrence group; the adapter assigns no `:decl` values (§8.5)                                                  |\n  1757\t+--------------------------------------------------+---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n  1758\t\n  1759\tThe v1 lineage (`src/cljc/yin/vm.cljc`, `test/yin/vm/ast_walker_test.cljc`,\n  1760\t`test/yin/vm/ast_conversion_test.cljc`, `runtime_regression_test.cljc`) is\n  1761\toutside this design; it stays on its own map path until it is retired.\n  1762\t\n  1763\t### 9.2 The observer lane: an integration item to scope, not a solved detail\n  1764\t\n  1765\tProgram input reaches an evaluator through\n  1766\t`dao.stream.observer/run-on-stream` (`src/cljc/dao/stream/observer.cljc:217-247`),\n  1767\twhich is shape-agnostic: it hands each observed batch to a\n  1768\tcomposition-supplied `load`. The per-evaluator loaders are chosen in\n  1769\t`yin.repl.core/program-loaders` (`repl/v2/core.cljc:63-68`) and today\n  1770\taccept datom batches; the REPL's own eval path still converts a\n  1771\tsemantic-VM AST through `ast->datoms` before it travels\n  1772\t(`repl/v2/core.cljc:444-449`). Two things about the row lane are\n  1773\t**unverified**:\n  1774\t\n  1775\t- **The batch shape.** Whether one batch is one tree's row set, one\n  1776\t  `{:yin/code :yin/declarations}` value (§8.5), one vector, or a\n  1777\t  collection of them is\n  1778\t  a medium contract this design has not exercised end to end; §7.1 assumes\n  1779\t  one canonical value per batch. The shape must be fixed with the REPL\n  1780\t  shell and the `yin.vm.stream-observer` tests before the loaders are\n  1781\t  switched.\n  1782\t- **The program-input predicate.** `engine/executable-program-datom?`\n  1783\t  (`engine.cljc:511-513`) decides what counts as program input by testing\n  1784\t  the attribute namespace of an `[e a v t m]` row, and\n  1785\t  `append-program-datoms` (`engine.cljc:590-612`) builds on it. A row\n  1786\t  batch has no attribute slot. A repository search on 2026-09-14 found no\n  1787\t  caller of the v2 `append-program-datoms` beyond its own overload, so a\n  1788\t  row counterpart is not presumed necessary; whether any live\n  1789\t  composition reaches the predicate is what the implementation phase\n  1790\t  establishes before deciding.\n  1791\t\n  1792\tBoth are integration items to scope in the implementation phase.\n  1793\t\n  1794\t### 9.3 What stays on datoms, and why\n  1795\t\n  1796\tThis is §1's datom-layer jobs\n  1797\tseen from the migration side:\n  1798\t\n  1799\t- **The ledger, every ref, and every ledger event** (§8): mutability,\n  1800\t  history, provenance, and custody are datom properties, and the\n  1801\t  transactor is what possesses them.\n  1802\t- **`dao.space.index` and published covered indexes**: they index datoms,\n  1803\t  and the scale path of §6.5 goes through them.\n  1804\t- **The macro expander's event log** (§8.4): events are entities with\n  1805\t  attributes, one per attempt; only their values changed kind.\n  1806\t- **The datom projection of code** (§6.5): kept for the three query classes\n  1807\t  it alone serves, regenerable from the content at any time.\n  1808\t- **Execution traces** (`yin.vm.semantic.md` §3.6): state over time is a\n  1809\t  datom stream by design and is untouched.\n  1810\t\n  1811\t---\n  1812\t\n  1813\t## §10 Acceptance blockers and open items\n  1814\t\n  1815\t1. **Type-preserving canonical encoding in `dao.jing`** — declared,\n  1816\t   inherited, and **blocking every identity and deduplication use**\n  1817\t   (§4.2). `order-normalize` (`jing.cljc:45-64`) coerces every sequential\n  1818\t   value to a vector and `pr-str` drops metadata, so lists, seqs, and\n  1819\t   metadata-bearing vectors collide with plain vectors. No restricted\n  1820\t   value domain is claimed; the round-2 list-rejection rule is withdrawn\n  1821\t   as unsound. Rows, vectors, ledger-record maps, row\n  1822\t   deduplication, DAG sharing, process-local caches, content resolution by\n  1823\t   address, and ledger rows are all blocked alike until `dao.jing`'s\n  1824\t   encoding passes the conformance pairs of §4.2. This design does not\n  1825\t   close the item.\n  1826\t2. **The store-key domain is the `data` domain** (§2.2): every validator,\n  1827\t   extraction query, and store-slice encoding must use that one\n  1828\t   definition. This document now states it uniformly; the implementation\n  1829\t   phase must not reintroduce a narrower one, and the corpus must contain\n  1830\t   a numeric key beside the keyword one.\n  1831\t3. **Complete occurrence identity** (§2.5, §5.3, §8.4) — design work\n  1832\t   with stated mechanisms, not yet implementable. Source origins\n  1833\t   `[:source medium batch j]` must be supplied to the lowering and\n  1834\t   recorded in instruction provenance and initial expansion events;\n  1835\t   nested expansion events must carry both the declared-ref parent link\n  1836\t   and the parent record address, and the record must be minted after\n  1837\t   the parent's. The medium identity coordinate is whatever the\n  1838\t   composition names its program medium by; this document does not fix\n  1839\t   its shape, and that shape must be fixed before an origin can be\n  1840\t   written. The expander incarnation (§8.4.1) is a composition-minted\n  1841\t   random token, never a log-allocated id or a stream position; its\n  1842\t   uniqueness is the composition's warranty, discharged by the token's\n  1843\t   randomness, and no transactor is claimed to provide it. The\n  1844\t   `:incarnation` field of `ctx` and the token-minting step at\n  1845\t   construction are additions to `yin.vm.macro.md`'s `ctx` and expander\n  1846\t   construction that this document cannot make there.\n  1847\t4. **Macro batch and admission preservation** (§8.5) — design work with\n  1848\t   stated mechanisms. The ordered batch, the admission-order harvest\n  1849\t   catalogue of occurrence groups, the derived macro-only declaration\n  1850\t   catalogue, its admission validation, the validate-before-strip rule,\n  1851\t   and the adapter's path-and-entity tracking during inlining must exist\n  1852\t   before any tuple program containing a macro definition can be\n  1853\t   admitted. The legacy datom-batch contract is\n  1854\t   preserved only through the adapter-derived catalogue; tuple-native\n  1855\t   frontends operate\n  1856\t   under the separate declaration-order contract stated there. The v2\n  1857\t   expander itself does not exist yet (`src/cljc/yin/vm/` has no\n  1858\t   `macro.cljc`) and is not a blocker for tuple evaluation without\n  1859\t   macros; §8.4's event shape is specified against `yin.vm.macro.md`, not\n  1860\t   against code.\n  1861\t5. **Conservative dependency completion** (§7.7.2, §7.7.3) — design\n  1862\t   work with stated mechanisms. Global name discharge requires searching the\n  1863\t   store slice by key or resolving via primitive/module profiles (§7.7.2);\n  1864\t   convergence is over work items and all dependency facts, with the\n  1865\t   positional-environment context abstraction for values. Neither analysis exists in code;\n  1866\t   until they do, an emitter may only report `:incomplete`.\n  1867\t6. **Effect normalization** (§7.7.1): the footprint table must be\n  1868\t   published with the execution-contract stamp, and the tree/segment\n  1869\t   requirement-set equality must be tested over the corpus. FFI ops are a\n  1870\t   receiver capability requirement, never a store-slice requirement.\n  1871\t7. **The lowering profile** (§5.2.1): `\"ast-v1\"` must be published as a\n  1872\t   pinned document before any derivation record is written; until then\n  1873\t   no derivation can be verified, only content integrity (§5.2.2).\n  1874\t8. **UCF §7.6.1 needs two amendments** (§7.7.2, §7.7.3): its\n  1875\t   any-carried-environment satisfaction sentence is unsound and must be\n  1876\t   replaced by the store-slice-by-key / profile rule of §7.7.2 (an environment never\n  1877\t   discharges a name), and its address-per-segment fixed\n  1878\t   point must become the work-item fixed point. This document cannot edit\n  1879\t   UCF.\n  1880\t9. **The observer-lane batch shape and the program-input predicate**\n  1881\t   (§9.2) — integration items with no design answer in this document. The\n  1882\t   §8.5 ordered batch is a candidate shape for the AST medium, not a\n  1883\t   verified one.\n  1884\t10. **Ledger event representation on the transactor** (§8.2): the\n  1885\t    event-entity form is legal under `local-datom?` as written, but the\n  1886\t    map-`m` provenance path (`transact.cljc:15-29`) and the metadata-op\n  1887\t    interpreter `dao.space.schema.datomic.md` §4 names are not exercised by\n  1888\t    this design's tests yet. The naming rows need neither; the provenance\n  1889\t    link from a naming fact to its event does.\n  1890\t11. **The primitive-profile registry** — inherited from UCF §7.11. Every\n  1891\t    retained name obligation is checked by profile under UCF §7.5.2, and\n  1892\t    §7.7.2 reads callable effects from profiles; until\n  1893\t    `yin.vm/primitives` (`v2.cljc:100-132`) is published with profiles,\n  1894\t    dependency closure reports names and `:incomplete`, not satisfiable\n  1895\t    bindings.\n  1896\t12. **Per-lambda subterm addressing** — dissolved by the flat-row form\n  1897\t    (§4.3): every node is addressed by construction, and the remaining\n  1898\t    one-segment-per-lambda lowering question is a §5.2.1 profile concern.\n  1899\t13. **The round-trip law** (§7.2, part 1) — a first-class conformance\n  1900\t    obligation, not a blocker: for every corpus program, `map → rows →\n  1901\t    map` is the identity on the canonical map AST and `rows → map →\n  1902\t    rows` is the identity on the rows, and both directions must be\n  1903\t    tested over the corpus before any row set is treated as canonical.\n  1904\t    The corpus already lives as map ASTs in the test tree, so the test\n  1905\t    is writable now.\n  1906\t14. **`yin.vm.semantic.md` Instruction Grammar Amendment** (§5.1) — the `ast-v1` lowering\n  1907\t    profile (§5.2.1) pins the §5.3 table of `yin.vm.semantic.md`, which is currently\n  1908\t    the name-based one. That document's §2.4 and §5.3 must be amended to reflect the\n  1909\t    Named variable instruction grammar\n  1910\t    and the positional binding rule of §7.7.2 (under-arity call → missing parameter names bound to `nil`; extra arguments beyond the params list length are dropped)\n  1911\t    before any derivation record is written.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
